1
00:00:26,036 --> 00:00:29,997
Blood. Blood. Blood. And death.

2
00:00:30,082 --> 00:00:31,583
All right. You know what?
You're milking it.

3
00:00:31,666 --> 00:00:33,376
Besides, I think we got it.

4
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:36,003
We got it up there.
Thank you, Yakkity-yak.

5
00:00:36,088 --> 00:00:37,380
You laid it all out beautifully.

6
00:00:37,464 --> 00:00:40,300
I framed Lionheart. I can frame you too.

7
00:00:40,383 --> 00:00:42,552
It's my word against yours.

8
00:00:42,635 --> 00:00:44,012
Actually...

9
00:00:44,096 --> 00:00:47,390
<i>And I'll dart every predator in Zootopia
to keep it that way.</i>

10
00:00:47,474 --> 00:00:49,392
It's your word against yours.

11
00:00:49,934 --> 00:00:52,062
It's called a hustle, sweetheart.

12
00:00:52,145 --> 00:00:53,146
Boom.

13
00:00:55,898 --> 00:00:56,899
{\an8}<i>Unlikely duo</i>

14
00:00:56,983 --> 00:00:59,944
{\an8}Judy Hopps, the city's first bunny cop,

15
00:01:00,028 --> 00:01:02,697
{\an8}and Nicholas Wilde,
a small-time street fox,

16
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:06,118
{\an8}<i>uncovered a conspiracy
by Mayor Bellwether today</i>

17
00:01:06,201 --> 00:01:08,370
<i>using a toy carrot recorder pen.</i>

18
00:01:08,453 --> 00:01:09,996
<i>I can frame you too.</i>

19
00:01:14,251 --> 00:01:17,379
<i>Wilde later joined the ZPD himself,</i>

20
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:20,382
<i>where the pair are now the very first
bunny-fox team</i>

21
00:01:20,465 --> 00:01:22,634
<i>in the history of Zootopia.</i>

22
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:27,972
{\an8}New mayor and former actor
Brian Winddancer hailed the two

23
00:01:28,056 --> 00:01:30,683
<i>during celebrations
for the 100th anniversary</i>

24
00:01:30,767 --> 00:01:33,603
<i>of the invention
of Zootopia's weather walls</i>

25
00:01:33,686 --> 00:01:36,023
<i>that allowed all environments
and all animals</i>

26
00:01:36,106 --> 00:01:38,358
<i>to coexist in one city.</i>

27
00:01:38,441 --> 00:01:41,778
This is our Zootennial!

28
00:01:41,861 --> 00:01:44,031
And if a lowly country bunny

29
00:01:44,114 --> 00:01:49,494
and a shifty, likely criminal fox
can ignore their vast, vast differences

30
00:01:49,577 --> 00:01:52,872
and solve bias and stereotype forever,

31
00:01:52,955 --> 00:01:57,669
then maybe we can all
embrace our differences

32
00:01:57,752 --> 00:02:00,422
and be better zoogether.

33
00:02:01,506 --> 00:02:04,384
We're gonna crack a new case,
make the world a better place,

34
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:06,678
and be the greatest partners of all time.

35
00:02:07,512 --> 00:02:09,181
I mean, we're not that different.

36
00:02:21,151 --> 00:02:27,407
<i>At the ZPD, partnership is
the cornerstone of success.</i>

37
00:02:27,490 --> 00:02:29,617
You want to put bad guys away?

38
00:02:29,701 --> 00:02:32,579
Be on the same page. Every day.

39
00:02:32,662 --> 00:02:35,873
Now today's bad guy is
this customs inspector,

40
00:02:35,957 --> 00:02:39,086
who's been smuggling
illegal overseas cargo

41
00:02:39,169 --> 00:02:40,545
through the city's shipyard.

42
00:02:40,628 --> 00:02:44,049
Captains Hoggbottom
and Truffler will lead.

43
00:02:44,132 --> 00:02:46,426
Higgins, Bloats, flank left.

44
00:02:46,509 --> 00:02:48,261
Chèvre, Bûcheron, right.

45
00:02:48,345 --> 00:02:51,431
-And down the middle, the Zebros.
-Zebros!

46
00:02:51,514 --> 00:02:53,891
Hopps and Wilde, I know you're eager
to prove yourselves,

47
00:02:53,975 --> 00:02:56,644
but as rookies, you will observe only

48
00:02:56,728 --> 00:03:00,523
and watch the veteran teams
show you how it's...

49
00:03:01,524 --> 00:03:02,692
Chief, hi.

50
00:03:02,775 --> 00:03:05,987
If you're looking for Nick and Judy,
they said they got it.

51
00:03:06,071 --> 00:03:09,782
And they are already on site,
with their baby!

52
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:19,876
Are you sure this'll work?

53
00:03:19,959 --> 00:03:21,836
You're the one that said we needed a bust.

54
00:03:21,919 --> 00:03:24,005
Just follow my lead, okay? Act casual.

55
00:03:24,089 --> 00:03:25,257
-Hey!
-Here we go.

56
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:26,424
Hello?

57
00:03:26,508 --> 00:03:27,967
This whole area's restricted.

58
00:03:28,051 --> 00:03:29,302
You can't be here.

59
00:03:30,178 --> 00:03:31,971
Fox and bunny. Okay.

60
00:03:32,055 --> 00:03:34,224
Yes, but proud parents first.

61
00:03:34,307 --> 00:03:36,393
-Mister...
-Inspector.

62
00:03:36,476 --> 00:03:37,769
Inspector Snootley.

63
00:03:37,852 --> 00:03:40,730
Inspector? What do you do?

64
00:03:40,813 --> 00:03:44,276
Ensure that nothing illegal gets smuggled
here in one of these cargo containers?

65
00:03:45,027 --> 00:03:47,112
That's a weird way to ask that.

66
00:03:47,195 --> 00:03:49,447
Anyway, you gotta leave.
You can't be here.

67
00:03:49,531 --> 00:03:51,866
Wait. Wait.

68
00:03:51,949 --> 00:03:53,701
He's right, gosh darn it.

69
00:03:54,202 --> 00:03:55,203
You are right, sir.

70
00:03:55,953 --> 00:03:58,498
I guess we should've thought this through.
Right, babe?

71
00:03:59,291 --> 00:04:02,502
Before we decided to come down here

72
00:04:02,585 --> 00:04:06,005
to this beautiful industrial shipyard

73
00:04:06,089 --> 00:04:11,719
to celebrate the big day
of our birthday boy.

74
00:04:11,803 --> 00:04:14,514
It's his birthday?

75
00:04:14,597 --> 00:04:16,432
Yeah. First one since...

76
00:04:17,517 --> 00:04:19,019
since the accident.

77
00:04:20,728 --> 00:04:23,648
And, you know, the one thing
this little stinker wished for,

78
00:04:23,731 --> 00:04:25,775
aside from one day
getting his tail reattached,

79
00:04:26,359 --> 00:04:29,529
was to see a choo-choo.

80
00:04:29,612 --> 00:04:33,075
And to maybe get a toot-toot conductor
to sign his cast.

81
00:04:33,158 --> 00:04:38,330
But I'm betting a customs inspector
would be even better.

82
00:04:38,871 --> 00:04:40,040
Really?

83
00:04:40,123 --> 00:04:42,084
All right. For the kid. For the kid.

84
00:04:42,584 --> 00:04:44,127
You're a saint.

85
00:04:44,211 --> 00:04:46,463
We thank you so much.
Here you go. Either leg.

86
00:04:46,546 --> 00:04:48,590
Or both. I don't care.

87
00:04:48,673 --> 00:04:49,966
Maybe put a doodle on it.

88
00:04:50,050 --> 00:04:51,093
Doesn't need to be perfect.

89
00:04:51,176 --> 00:04:52,969
He's also legally blind.

90
00:04:58,641 --> 00:05:01,269
I put a little train there
because I know you like trains.

91
00:05:01,353 --> 00:05:02,937
Come on. Come on. Come on.

92
00:05:03,438 --> 00:05:04,897
-Just jiggle it.
-Yes. I know.

93
00:05:04,981 --> 00:05:05,815
-Hurry!
-I got this.

94
00:05:05,898 --> 00:05:07,734
Jiggle it. Jiggle it. Jiggle it!

95
00:05:08,235 --> 00:05:11,238
<i>Hopps and Wilde, you are not authorized.</i>

96
00:05:11,321 --> 00:05:13,323
<i>Stand down and wait for backup.</i>

97
00:05:17,494 --> 00:05:18,495
Toot, toot.

98
00:05:18,578 --> 00:05:20,247
-It's the fuzz!
-Stop!

99
00:05:20,330 --> 00:05:23,333
-Stop in the name of the law!
-Everybody run!

100
00:05:25,835 --> 00:05:27,879
Well, can't win 'em all.

101
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:30,382
Get outta the road, you dumb bunny.

102
00:05:30,965 --> 00:05:32,342
Agree to disagree.

103
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:36,929
My hog rod! This is a snoutrage!

104
00:05:39,682 --> 00:05:41,809
Hopps and Wilde in pursuit of suspect

105
00:05:41,893 --> 00:05:44,146
in a stolen catering van heading east...

106
00:05:44,229 --> 00:05:45,272
Stop it.

107
00:05:45,355 --> 00:05:47,232
...heading east through Sheepshire.

108
00:06:01,121 --> 00:06:02,122
Beautiful.

109
00:06:02,205 --> 00:06:03,956
I'll have what he's having.

110
00:06:04,041 --> 00:06:07,544
Darling, I believe your driving is
giving me some white hairs.

111
00:06:08,086 --> 00:06:10,380
Also, am I always gonna be
in the passenger's seat?

112
00:06:10,463 --> 00:06:12,215
'Cause if we're establishing sides...

113
00:06:12,299 --> 00:06:14,384
Back off, rookies!

114
00:06:14,467 --> 00:06:16,178
Let the real teams handle it.

115
00:06:16,261 --> 00:06:20,015
-Ma'am, we are a real team.
-Shortcut. Take the tunnel.

116
00:06:30,358 --> 00:06:31,776
<i>Stand down, noobs.</i>

117
00:06:31,859 --> 00:06:32,860
We got him.

118
00:06:32,944 --> 00:06:34,737
-Rolling spikes!
-Rolling spikes!

119
00:06:45,665 --> 00:06:47,500
I hate that dumb bunny.

120
00:07:10,357 --> 00:07:11,691
-I'm gonna jump.
-What?

121
00:07:11,774 --> 00:07:12,734
No, no. What are you...

122
00:07:12,817 --> 00:07:16,488
Carrots, hey... I would like to call
a partner meeting. No, Carrots!

123
00:07:17,322 --> 00:07:18,323
Judy!

124
00:07:27,624 --> 00:07:29,626
Please join me in a moment of silence

125
00:07:29,709 --> 00:07:33,546
for our beloved weather wall inventor
Ebenezer Lynxley.

126
00:07:38,676 --> 00:07:39,969
Zebros!

127
00:07:54,942 --> 00:07:56,361
Reptile?

128
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,117
Chief, those two are out of control!

129
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,994
<i>Zootopia ain't just a mammal city.</i>

130
00:08:05,495 --> 00:08:09,499
<i>It has a secret, tiny reptile population,</i>

131
00:08:10,042 --> 00:08:11,834
<i>though most live abroad.</i>

132
00:08:11,918 --> 00:08:14,879
<i>Of course, Zootopia does not allow snakes.</i>

133
00:08:15,838 --> 00:08:19,509
<i>There ain't been one of those around here
for at least a hundred years.</i>

134
00:08:20,010 --> 00:08:22,512
<i>"Scales and tales of the weird."</i>

135
00:08:22,595 --> 00:08:28,143
Yeah, not sure Nibbles Maplestick
is our most reliable source.

136
00:08:28,226 --> 00:08:31,354
Bunny, orange dog, in here now.

137
00:08:32,522 --> 00:08:35,482
Sir, today may not have been ideal,
but the anteater was captured.

138
00:08:35,567 --> 00:08:36,818
By the Zebros!

139
00:08:36,901 --> 00:08:38,403
-Zebros!
-Shut it!

140
00:08:39,071 --> 00:08:40,322
Zebros.

141
00:08:40,405 --> 00:08:41,406
It was us.

142
00:08:41,488 --> 00:08:45,660
But more importantly, I believe we may
have made a significant discovery.

143
00:08:45,743 --> 00:08:48,871
The stolen van contained a smuggled crate
from overseas,

144
00:08:48,955 --> 00:08:50,248
pamphlets for the Zootennial,

145
00:08:50,332 --> 00:08:52,167
and some type of reptile skin.

146
00:08:52,250 --> 00:08:53,835
I've already sent samples for testing.

147
00:08:53,918 --> 00:08:56,504
-And if you...
-What needs testing is you!

148
00:08:56,588 --> 00:08:58,923
You two tore up half the city.

149
00:08:59,006 --> 00:09:01,551
Because of you,
we had to call in Jumbo Unit

150
00:09:01,634 --> 00:09:03,636
to remove a dik-dik from a tuba!

151
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:07,265
<i>-You're almost out. You're gonna be...
-No! Please, no!</i>

152
00:09:07,349 --> 00:09:08,975
<i>Who's catching him?</i>

153
00:09:09,059 --> 00:09:12,062
Sorry, could you show me that clip again?
Wasn't wearing my glasses.

154
00:09:12,645 --> 00:09:16,691
Did you or did you not disobey
a direct order to stand down?

155
00:09:16,774 --> 00:09:18,235
Sir, we were in pursuit.

156
00:09:18,318 --> 00:09:21,446
And article 6, paragraph B states,
"If the lead officers think..."

157
00:09:21,529 --> 00:09:23,198
You are not lead officers!

158
00:09:23,281 --> 00:09:24,574
You're one-hit wonders

159
00:09:24,657 --> 00:09:27,994
who should go back to meter maiding
and slinging pawpsicles!

160
00:09:28,078 --> 00:09:32,457
If I may, I think someone's just jealous
that we got to drive the squeal mobile.

161
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:35,127
Or maybe you thought it was your momma.

162
00:09:35,627 --> 00:09:38,213
Enough! Everybody out.

163
00:09:43,468 --> 00:09:44,719
Officer Hopps,

164
00:09:44,802 --> 00:09:48,806
despite my best efforts to avoid it,
I like you.

165
00:09:49,391 --> 00:09:54,271
But this need to overdo it

166
00:09:54,354 --> 00:09:57,190
made both of you a headline today.

167
00:09:57,857 --> 00:10:01,403
And it reflects badly on me,
on the department,

168
00:10:01,486 --> 00:10:07,200
and frankly on any bunny hoping
to follow in your footsteps.

169
00:10:09,161 --> 00:10:11,954
Not every case is going to save the world.

170
00:10:13,706 --> 00:10:14,707
Knock-knock.

171
00:10:15,208 --> 00:10:16,209
Hi.

172
00:10:16,293 --> 00:10:19,921
You know, this kinda... this sounds a lot
like a... just a "you guys" conversation.

173
00:10:20,004 --> 00:10:21,881
So what I'm gonna do
is I'm gonna go ahead and...

174
00:10:21,964 --> 00:10:24,926
Is there a reason why you don't
take anything seriously?

175
00:10:25,010 --> 00:10:27,762
Jokes are a classic defense mechanism
for someone with a traumatic childhood.

176
00:10:27,845 --> 00:10:29,764
Would you like a traumatic adulthood?

177
00:10:30,765 --> 00:10:31,766
I would not.

178
00:10:31,849 --> 00:10:36,313
I allowed you to work together
because you did this city a great service.

179
00:10:36,396 --> 00:10:39,399
But today, you messed it all up.

180
00:10:39,482 --> 00:10:40,483
And now,

181
00:10:40,567 --> 00:10:43,403
some are questioning whether you should've
been partners in the first place.

182
00:10:43,486 --> 00:10:44,612
-Sir...
-So,

183
00:10:44,696 --> 00:10:47,199
instead of chasing imaginary reptiles,

184
00:10:47,282 --> 00:10:48,825
I am pulling you from the field...

185
00:10:48,908 --> 00:10:51,578
-What?
-...and giving you a new assignment

186
00:10:51,661 --> 00:10:55,165
reserved for special teams
like yourselves.

187
00:10:55,248 --> 00:11:00,212
Fail at this, and I will have no choice
but to split you up.

188
00:11:00,837 --> 00:11:02,964
There will be no more Hopps and Wilde.

189
00:11:03,590 --> 00:11:07,594
But if you're as good
as you think you are,

190
00:11:08,386 --> 00:11:10,472
this is your moment to shine.

191
00:11:13,016 --> 00:11:16,186
Welcome to Partners in Crisis,

192
00:11:16,269 --> 00:11:19,606
a workshop for duos heading for disaster.

193
00:11:19,689 --> 00:11:22,525
I'm your therapy animal, Dr. Fuzzby.

194
00:11:22,609 --> 00:11:23,610
And you are here

195
00:11:23,693 --> 00:11:27,405
because mismatched pairings
often need a little more work

196
00:11:27,489 --> 00:11:29,407
to make them work.

197
00:11:29,491 --> 00:11:31,868
Greg, we talked about that.

198
00:11:31,951 --> 00:11:33,161
What are we making Marlon?

199
00:11:33,245 --> 00:11:35,622
Uncomfortable. Yes.

200
00:11:36,581 --> 00:11:40,960
And, Francine, Clark may be a mouse,
but he is your partner first.

201
00:11:42,129 --> 00:11:44,756
And we have two new "apart-ners."

202
00:11:45,340 --> 00:11:49,677
Can you tell us about your journey
to dysfunction junction?

203
00:11:49,761 --> 00:11:53,890
Well, we've been official partners
for one week.

204
00:11:53,973 --> 00:11:55,767
Happy anniversary.

205
00:11:55,850 --> 00:11:58,395
And we're not dysfunctional
at all, actually.

206
00:11:58,478 --> 00:12:00,647
Functioning fine. Better than fine.

207
00:12:00,730 --> 00:12:02,649
And we did sort of save the city,

208
00:12:02,732 --> 00:12:06,361
so us being here kinda seems like
a huge misunderstanding.

209
00:12:07,404 --> 00:12:10,573
Notice how she answered first,
did not allow her partner to speak,

210
00:12:10,657 --> 00:12:11,949
seems to be in a state of denial

211
00:12:12,034 --> 00:12:14,452
and taps her foot
when she's suppressing discomfort.

212
00:12:15,328 --> 00:12:17,330
And observe the source of her discomfort

213
00:12:17,414 --> 00:12:19,332
represented by
the disconnected affectation

214
00:12:19,416 --> 00:12:21,459
of her emotionally insecure partner.

215
00:12:22,001 --> 00:12:25,880
But with hard work,
they'll be on the same page by year two.

216
00:12:25,963 --> 00:12:29,509
But for now,
let's begin by greeting our teammate.

217
00:12:29,592 --> 00:12:31,053
Who'd like to go first?

218
00:12:31,136 --> 00:12:32,262
Thank you, Joel.

219
00:12:35,640 --> 00:12:41,021
Okay, unfortunately, Karen has chosen to
see Joel's smile as a sign of aggression.

220
00:12:41,104 --> 00:12:43,856
Hackles down, Karen. Karen, hackles down.

221
00:12:43,940 --> 00:12:46,068
Joel, the safe word is "coconut."

222
00:12:46,151 --> 00:12:47,402
Coconut!

223
00:12:49,446 --> 00:12:51,614
Okay, we have to set
another meeting with the Chief.

224
00:12:51,698 --> 00:12:53,450
Promise we'll be better.

225
00:12:54,701 --> 00:12:56,119
Zebros!

226
00:12:56,869 --> 00:12:59,081
You talk back less. Maybe not at all.

227
00:12:59,164 --> 00:13:03,210
Yeah. Or we go with my playbook,
and we just lay low.

228
00:13:03,293 --> 00:13:05,837
We cannot prove ourselves
if we are stuck in that room.

229
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:08,881
And if we complain to Chief Beef,
he'll just make us stay longer.

230
00:13:08,965 --> 00:13:10,675
Play it smart. Stay off the radar.

231
00:13:10,758 --> 00:13:12,594
And don't let it get to you. Okay?

232
00:13:12,677 --> 00:13:14,762
'Cause we are the dream team.

233
00:13:17,140 --> 00:13:18,141
Sorry, Paul.

234
00:13:22,687 --> 00:13:24,981
Roll out the red carpet.
Here's the super cops.

235
00:13:25,732 --> 00:13:27,192
Hey, what does the fox say?

236
00:13:27,275 --> 00:13:28,276
You suck!

237
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:29,569
Easy, partner.

238
00:13:29,652 --> 00:13:31,029
Let me ask you something.

239
00:13:31,113 --> 00:13:32,572
Do you think we're a good team?

240
00:13:32,655 --> 00:13:34,032
Of course.

241
00:13:34,116 --> 00:13:35,283
I just...

242
00:13:35,367 --> 00:13:38,995
I just wish I knew what we had to do
to prove it to everyone else.

243
00:13:39,079 --> 00:13:42,540
Or maybe we don't have
to prove anything to anybody.

244
00:13:45,543 --> 00:13:47,337
We do. Forever.

245
00:13:47,420 --> 00:13:48,880
And the sooner everyone sees

246
00:13:48,963 --> 00:13:52,509
a fox and a bunny can be
great partners, the better. Enjoy it.

247
00:13:52,592 --> 00:13:55,053
-I got two.
-I'd rather chew off my own arm.

248
00:13:55,137 --> 00:13:56,929
Happy anniversary!

249
00:14:06,356 --> 00:14:07,357
Coconut!

250
00:14:31,423 --> 00:14:34,676
<i>So, saw you were on TV. You okay?</i>

251
00:14:34,759 --> 00:14:36,261
Everything's fine, Mom.

252
00:14:36,344 --> 00:14:38,971
<i>Uh-oh. Everyone knows
"fine" is a cry for help.</i>

253
00:14:39,056 --> 00:14:41,641
<i>You know who else cried for help?
That dik-dik stuck in that tuba.</i>

254
00:14:41,724 --> 00:14:44,602
<i>Him and the dik-dik.
What does your fox partner say?</i>

255
00:14:44,686 --> 00:14:48,606
<i>Well, Nick's not really a talker.</i>

256
00:14:49,191 --> 00:14:51,776
<i>Well, you two are very different.</i>

257
00:14:51,859 --> 00:14:54,112
<i>Small-town hero raised on a farm.</i>

258
00:14:54,196 --> 00:14:55,988
<i>Big-city crook raised on the streets.</i>

259
00:14:56,073 --> 00:14:58,908
<i>I pray every day that you'll come
back home and make babies.</i>

260
00:14:58,991 --> 00:15:00,035
<i>Mom!</i>

261
00:15:00,118 --> 00:15:01,494
<i>Just remember the first rule
of partnerships.</i>

262
00:15:01,578 --> 00:15:03,996
<i>-You can be right or you can be happy.
-That's true.</i>

263
00:15:04,081 --> 00:15:06,541
<i>There's nothing wrong
with dying a little inside</i>

264
00:15:06,624 --> 00:15:07,459
<i>so you can meet in the middle.</i>

265
00:15:07,542 --> 00:15:09,919
<i>-Honey, you're pushing me out.
-I wanna make sure I can be seen.</i>

266
00:15:10,002 --> 00:15:12,089
<i>-Well, if she could see both of us.</i>
-Gotta go. Work to do.

267
00:15:12,172 --> 00:15:13,756
<i>-Bye, bun-bun.
-Everything's gonna be fine.</i>

268
00:15:13,840 --> 00:15:15,633
<i>-You two just need a new case.</i>
-Got it. Love you.

269
00:15:15,717 --> 00:15:17,927
<i>-Maybe try to find that dik-dik.</i>
-Bye.

270
00:15:30,773 --> 00:15:32,692
<i>Squeal of Fortune!</i>

271
00:15:32,775 --> 00:15:35,612
<i>Tomorrow's weather is, again, everything.</i>

272
00:15:35,695 --> 00:15:36,738
{\an8}<i>...to announce that</i>

273
00:15:36,821 --> 00:15:40,158
{\an8}<i>the long-awaited Tundratown expansion
will be starting soon.</i>

274
00:15:40,242 --> 00:15:43,995
<i>Residents continue to raise concerns
about the future of their neighborhood.</i>

275
00:15:44,079 --> 00:15:45,080
<i>But tonight,</i>

276
00:15:45,163 --> 00:15:49,792
<i>we put our differences aside
and celebrate at the Zootennial gala,</i>

277
00:15:49,876 --> 00:15:54,672
<i>where the Lynxley Journal will be on
display for the first time in a century,</i>

278
00:15:54,756 --> 00:15:57,259
<i>following an infamous reptile attack.</i>

279
00:15:57,884 --> 00:16:01,054
{\an8}<i>The Lynxley Journal, containing
the patented weather wall plans,</i>

280
00:16:01,138 --> 00:16:04,932
<i>was originally written by
Ebenezer Lynxley, bringing...</i>

281
00:16:19,656 --> 00:16:21,324
Same catering company.

282
00:16:34,254 --> 00:16:35,755
The Lynxley journal.

283
00:16:43,971 --> 00:16:45,515
<i>Truth bomb!</i>

284
00:16:45,598 --> 00:16:49,852
<i>The 100th anniversary of the weather walls
is also the 100th anniversary</i>

285
00:16:49,936 --> 00:16:52,355
<i>of Zootopia's only snake attack.</i>

286
00:16:52,939 --> 00:16:54,982
<i>The victim? A tortoise.</i>

287
00:16:55,067 --> 00:17:00,780
<i>The Lynxley family maid who was fanged
while stopping a venomous snake</i>

288
00:17:00,863 --> 00:17:03,283
<i>from stealing the Lynxley Journal.</i>

289
00:17:03,366 --> 00:17:05,993
<i>No one has seen a snake in Zootopia since.</i>

290
00:17:06,077 --> 00:17:09,580
<i>But I'm convinced one will return.</i>

291
00:17:09,664 --> 00:17:11,291
<i>My only question is,</i>

292
00:17:11,374 --> 00:17:16,462
<i>is he gonna be wearing
half-a-pants or one long sock?</i>

293
00:17:21,634 --> 00:17:24,179
<i>You say, "Justice is dead."</i>

294
00:17:24,262 --> 00:17:26,389
<i>I say, "Neigh!"</i>

295
00:17:31,436 --> 00:17:33,146
Well, well, you miss me already, huh?

296
00:17:33,230 --> 00:17:36,274
<i>The stolen van with the snake skin is
from the same catering company</i>

297
00:17:36,358 --> 00:17:37,859
<i>that's working the gala.</i>

298
00:17:37,942 --> 00:17:39,736
This is you studying your partner book?

299
00:17:39,819 --> 00:17:43,406
<i>Listen to me. Venomous snakes tried
to steal the Lynxleys's journal before.</i>

300
00:17:43,490 --> 00:17:45,283
<i>What if they're back in town
to steal it again?</i>

301
00:17:45,367 --> 00:17:49,537
No snake has set foot in Zootopia
in forever, okay?

302
00:17:49,621 --> 00:17:50,788
Even if they had feet.

303
00:17:51,289 --> 00:17:54,167
Look, we're already in the hot seat,
Carrots, and pizza's here. Gotta go--

304
00:17:54,251 --> 00:17:56,503
If there's a fanging at the gala
and we do nothing to stop it,

305
00:17:56,586 --> 00:17:58,421
then we are not doing our jobs…

306
00:17:58,505 --> 00:18:01,299
Yikes. No wonder you never invite me over.

307
00:18:01,383 --> 00:18:02,884
Foxes are solitary.

308
00:18:02,967 --> 00:18:06,221
And, look, we go and you're wrong,
Chief Beef will split us up.

309
00:18:06,304 --> 00:18:08,723
And if we don't get a win soon
and prove we're great partners,

310
00:18:08,806 --> 00:18:10,725
the chief will split us up anyway.

311
00:18:10,808 --> 00:18:12,269
Check the perimeter. Snoop a little.

312
00:18:12,352 --> 00:18:14,104
We find nothing, we bail.

313
00:18:14,187 --> 00:18:18,566
But if I'm right, no one will question
whether you and I belong together again.

314
00:18:19,317 --> 00:18:20,485
We need this.

315
00:18:21,903 --> 00:18:22,945
Fine.

316
00:18:23,030 --> 00:18:25,490
But we'll still have
to get through security,

317
00:18:25,573 --> 00:18:29,411
and in fancy town,
a fox and a bunny won't exactly blend in.

318
00:18:29,494 --> 00:18:31,746
That's why we'll be undercover!

319
00:18:35,875 --> 00:18:37,835
See you're still in the driver's seat.

320
00:18:37,919 --> 00:18:40,130
<i>Come on, get on up</i>

321
00:18:40,213 --> 00:18:42,174
<i>We're wild and we can't be tamed</i>

322
00:18:42,257 --> 00:18:45,260
<i>And we're turnin' the floor
Into a zoo-ooh-ooh</i>

323
00:18:45,343 --> 00:18:47,262
Here we go! Here we go! Let's go!

324
00:18:48,763 --> 00:18:50,640
Zootopia, let me hear ya!

325
00:18:53,060 --> 00:18:56,813
<i>We live in a crazy world
Caught up in a rat race</i>

326
00:18:56,896 --> 00:19:00,275
<i>Concrete jungle life
Is sometimes a mad place</i>

327
00:19:00,358 --> 00:19:03,778
<i>It's you and me together
At the end of a wild day</i>

328
00:19:03,861 --> 00:19:07,532
<i>Don't keep it all bottled up
And release your energy</i>

329
00:19:09,201 --> 00:19:12,579
<i>Only reason we are here is to celebrate</i>

330
00:19:13,121 --> 00:19:16,708
<i>In a place where anyone can be anything</i>

331
00:19:16,791 --> 00:19:20,170
<i>Hold on to this moment
Don't let it fade away</i>

332
00:19:20,253 --> 00:19:22,630
<i>Baby, keep the music playin'</i>

333
00:19:22,714 --> 00:19:24,549
<i>Come on, get on up</i>

334
00:19:24,632 --> 00:19:26,926
<i>We're wild and we can't be tamed</i>

335
00:19:27,010 --> 00:19:29,596
<i>And we're turnin' the floor
Into a zoo-ooh-ooh</i>

336
00:19:30,222 --> 00:19:32,099
<i>Come on, keep it up</i>

337
00:19:32,182 --> 00:19:34,392
<i>It's fun if you're down to play</i>

338
00:19:34,476 --> 00:19:37,312
<i>And we're turnin' the floor
Into a zoo-ooh-ooh</i>

339
00:19:40,773 --> 00:19:43,110
Cats and lights. I know better.

340
00:19:43,193 --> 00:19:44,986
<i>A zoo-ooh-ooh</i>

341
00:19:45,070 --> 00:19:46,779
<i>Come on, get on up</i>

342
00:19:46,863 --> 00:19:49,282
<i>We're wild and we can't be tamed</i>

343
00:19:49,366 --> 00:19:51,951
<i>And we're turnin' the floor
Into a zoo-ooh-ooh</i>

344
00:19:52,535 --> 00:19:54,496
And Mayor Winddancer has arrived,

345
00:19:55,247 --> 00:19:57,790
star of the revenge trilogy,
<i>The Neigh-sayer</i>.

346
00:19:57,874 --> 00:19:59,376
No pictures, please.

347
00:20:00,168 --> 00:20:01,419
Come on. It's me.

348
00:20:01,503 --> 00:20:03,880
And that, and that one, and then this one.

349
00:20:03,963 --> 00:20:06,758
Mr. Lynxley, when will you begin
the Tundratown expansion?

350
00:20:06,841 --> 00:20:09,886
-Come on, Dad.
-Have a wonderful time at the party.

351
00:20:09,969 --> 00:20:11,513
<i>It's fun if you're down to play</i>

352
00:20:11,596 --> 00:20:14,891
<i>And we're turnin' the floor
Into a zoo-ooh-ooh</i>

353
00:20:31,241 --> 00:20:32,700
No snow.

354
00:20:34,077 --> 00:20:35,370
This one's warmer.

355
00:20:35,870 --> 00:20:38,873
A reptile in Tundratown might need that
to survive the cold.

356
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:42,502
Oh, my God. A viper!

357
00:20:43,045 --> 00:20:45,755
A window wiper. <i>Ja?</i>

358
00:20:50,302 --> 00:20:51,761
Back door ajar.

359
00:20:51,844 --> 00:20:53,346
Anyone could have left it like that.

360
00:20:53,430 --> 00:20:55,640
And you said, "Snoop a little,"
not break and enter--

361
00:20:55,723 --> 00:20:56,724
Oh, God!

362
00:20:57,559 --> 00:21:00,853
We were always going inside.
Got it. Same page means your page.

363
00:21:00,937 --> 00:21:02,439
It's called a hustle, sweetheart.

364
00:21:02,522 --> 00:21:04,107
You change out here. I get the van.

365
00:21:21,166 --> 00:21:22,875
It's the Zootennial Gala.

366
00:21:22,959 --> 00:21:24,419
A bunny comes prepared.

367
00:21:27,172 --> 00:21:30,550
You know, I used to dream
of infiltrating a place like this.

368
00:21:37,140 --> 00:21:40,143
You know, this is not your worst idea.

369
00:21:40,852 --> 00:21:42,729
Wow, that was almost a compliment.

370
00:21:43,230 --> 00:21:45,273
Your worst idea
is what you did with your ears.

371
00:21:47,109 --> 00:21:48,110
All right.

372
00:22:00,372 --> 00:22:03,625
<i>Welcome to the Zootennial Gala,</i>

373
00:22:03,708 --> 00:22:07,212
<i>featuring the original Lynxley Journal.</i>

374
00:22:07,295 --> 00:22:11,383
Why would a snake wanna steal
some old book anyway?

375
00:22:11,466 --> 00:22:12,759
I don't know.

376
00:22:14,011 --> 00:22:17,347
But no one is gonna steal it,
because we're here to protect it.

377
00:22:20,975 --> 00:22:24,271
The journal is
in the reserved section. Okay.

378
00:22:25,897 --> 00:22:26,731
Come on.

379
00:22:26,814 --> 00:22:27,815
Slow down.

380
00:22:27,899 --> 00:22:31,653
You want to fit in?
Commando is not gonna cut it.

381
00:22:31,736 --> 00:22:33,655
It's not just the clothes, m'kay?

382
00:22:33,738 --> 00:22:35,365
It is the vibe.

383
00:22:35,448 --> 00:22:38,118
Good to see ya. Hey, real nice humps.

384
00:22:38,660 --> 00:22:40,662
Is that real mink? This is real fox.

385
00:22:41,329 --> 00:22:43,165
Get invited to a lot of these?

386
00:22:44,041 --> 00:22:45,417
Invited? No.

387
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:47,960
But there's other reasons to hob knob.

388
00:22:49,754 --> 00:22:52,006
Madam, excuse me. Is this yours?

389
00:22:52,090 --> 00:22:53,633
Why, yes.

390
00:22:53,716 --> 00:22:55,718
Well, allow me.

391
00:22:58,180 --> 00:22:59,764
Clever fox.

392
00:22:59,847 --> 00:23:00,848
There!

393
00:23:02,434 --> 00:23:04,186
I don't think it has enough guards.

394
00:23:04,811 --> 00:23:06,854
I'll get a closer look. Watch my six.

395
00:23:13,653 --> 00:23:14,654
Bogo alert.

396
00:23:28,376 --> 00:23:30,212
Cover's made of metal.

397
00:23:30,295 --> 00:23:32,672
<i>Gruffalo Buffalo has joined the mix.</i>

398
00:23:32,755 --> 00:23:33,756
<i>Three o'clock.</i>

399
00:23:42,599 --> 00:23:45,185
-Excuse me. I am so sorry.
-Oh, no. Sorry.

400
00:23:45,268 --> 00:23:47,104
Here, lemme just… Oh, nope.

401
00:23:47,187 --> 00:23:49,439
That belongs to you. I apologize.

402
00:23:50,232 --> 00:23:51,816
Oh, gosh. He hates me.

403
00:23:51,899 --> 00:23:53,276
Here.

404
00:23:53,360 --> 00:23:54,361
You're my hero.

405
00:23:54,444 --> 00:23:57,989
Well, just like to be prepared
for any possible scenario.

406
00:23:58,073 --> 00:24:00,867
Which is not a weird thing
to say at a party.

407
00:24:00,950 --> 00:24:03,995
Well, I just mopped a spill
with that guy's tail, so…

408
00:24:04,662 --> 00:24:05,663
Paw!

409
00:24:07,582 --> 00:24:10,001
Pawbert. I am Pawbert. Hi.

410
00:24:10,085 --> 00:24:11,628
Judy. I'm Judy Hopps.

411
00:24:12,212 --> 00:24:13,421
What the pork?

412
00:24:13,505 --> 00:24:15,507
Well, 'tis nice to meet you, Judy…

413
00:24:15,590 --> 00:24:18,051
Wait. Judy Hopps? The Judy Hopps?

414
00:24:18,135 --> 00:24:19,844
Are you working this?

415
00:24:19,927 --> 00:24:21,638
I mean, you're clearly working it.

416
00:24:22,180 --> 00:24:23,890
But are you actually working this?

417
00:24:24,849 --> 00:24:27,435
No. Actually… Well, it's just a hunch.

418
00:24:27,519 --> 00:24:28,936
Protecting that.

419
00:24:29,729 --> 00:24:32,149
Good. I don't think it has enough guards.

420
00:24:32,982 --> 00:24:34,442
<i>That was my joke.</i>

421
00:24:34,526 --> 00:24:36,069
Yeah, and there's nothing here.

422
00:24:36,153 --> 00:24:37,737
Let's call it a night.

423
00:24:37,820 --> 00:24:38,821
Hopps?

424
00:24:45,078 --> 00:24:46,204
Well, we should…

425
00:24:46,288 --> 00:24:47,330
It was nice to meet you.

426
00:24:47,414 --> 00:24:49,082
Yes, and you.

427
00:24:49,166 --> 00:24:50,167
Bon appétit.

428
00:24:50,250 --> 00:24:54,462
If, at any point tonight,
you choose to eat, bon appétit then.

429
00:24:54,546 --> 00:24:56,756
You're supposed to be off stage, Pawbert.

430
00:24:56,839 --> 00:24:58,966
Yeah, get off stage, Pawbert.

431
00:24:59,051 --> 00:25:00,177
Wait, are you…

432
00:25:00,260 --> 00:25:02,804
Yeah, I am a Lynxley.

433
00:25:02,887 --> 00:25:04,389
Or, you know, I'm trying to be.

434
00:25:04,472 --> 00:25:05,557
Now, Pawbert.

435
00:25:05,640 --> 00:25:06,724
Come on, Papi.

436
00:25:06,808 --> 00:25:08,560
-I got you, Pa. Don't you worry.
-No, I have him.

437
00:25:08,643 --> 00:25:11,604
All right, let's get this thing started.

438
00:25:16,109 --> 00:25:18,361
Welcome to the Zootennial Gala.

439
00:25:18,445 --> 00:25:20,947
Hey, Hopps. I think I might actually…

440
00:25:21,031 --> 00:25:22,199
I think I've got something.

441
00:25:23,491 --> 00:25:24,576
Look at you all.

442
00:25:25,493 --> 00:25:27,079
Good to see ya.

443
00:25:27,162 --> 00:25:31,166
It is with great pleasure that I introduce
the third-generation visionary…

444
00:25:31,249 --> 00:25:33,793
Carrots, can you hear me? I have a trail.

445
00:25:33,876 --> 00:25:38,090
…who has continued the legacy
of our great city, Milton Lynxley.

446
00:25:38,173 --> 00:25:40,925
-Thank you. Thank you very much.
-Carrots. Carrots.

447
00:25:42,844 --> 00:25:44,554
What are you doing here?

448
00:25:44,637 --> 00:25:45,638
Chief, she was right.

449
00:25:45,722 --> 00:25:47,724
-You're done. You're both done.
-Wait. No, no.

450
00:25:47,807 --> 00:25:51,436
My grandpa had a dream
to build a city for all animals.

451
00:25:51,519 --> 00:25:52,687
Nick?

452
00:25:53,271 --> 00:25:57,400
To truly be better together.

453
00:26:07,994 --> 00:26:09,454
There's a snake!

454
00:26:09,537 --> 00:26:10,580
Snake, snake, snake!

455
00:26:11,248 --> 00:26:12,582
Go, bro, go.

456
00:26:12,665 --> 00:26:14,501
I will remember you, zebra.

457
00:26:38,441 --> 00:26:39,609
Judy. Judy!

458
00:26:45,115 --> 00:26:46,116
I knew it!

459
00:27:04,551 --> 00:27:05,552
Stop!

460
00:27:07,095 --> 00:27:09,222
Please. You don't have to hurt him.

461
00:27:10,723 --> 00:27:11,724
Hurt him?

462
00:27:12,267 --> 00:27:16,688
Snakes never hurt anyone.

463
00:27:16,771 --> 00:27:18,648
We aren't the bad guys.

464
00:27:19,399 --> 00:27:20,650
They are.

465
00:27:21,901 --> 00:27:26,489
And this journal holds the secret
that will prove it.

466
00:27:27,199 --> 00:27:31,203
I have to prove it. Please.

467
00:27:33,330 --> 00:27:36,541
This is our only chance
to set things right.

468
00:27:37,042 --> 00:27:38,835
And when I do,

469
00:27:38,918 --> 00:27:43,381
my family will finally be able
to come home.

470
00:27:44,716 --> 00:27:46,968
-Oh, no!
-I am here.

471
00:27:47,052 --> 00:27:48,886
Hopps and Wilde, dream team.

472
00:27:49,429 --> 00:27:51,431
We got him. Or her.

473
00:27:51,514 --> 00:27:53,433
Reptiles, it's, like, who knows?

474
00:27:55,143 --> 00:27:56,644
The cops are right behind us.

475
00:27:57,979 --> 00:27:59,439
Kill the snake.

476
00:27:59,522 --> 00:28:03,360
We'll burn the journal.
If he wants it, it's dangerous.

477
00:28:04,527 --> 00:28:08,990
And you two will file a report
that he attacked us,

478
00:28:09,074 --> 00:28:12,702
and you will keep your mouths shut.

479
00:28:15,330 --> 00:28:16,164
Step aside.

480
00:28:20,335 --> 00:28:21,794
Put out the fire!

481
00:28:22,962 --> 00:28:24,422
-Carrots, come on.
-Help me save him.

482
00:28:24,506 --> 00:28:26,341
-Carrots, we need--
-Nick.

483
00:28:26,966 --> 00:28:28,885
Cold! Cold!

484
00:28:31,513 --> 00:28:32,472
No!

485
00:28:36,018 --> 00:28:37,060
Chief.

486
00:28:37,144 --> 00:28:38,853
He'll be okay.

487
00:28:38,936 --> 00:28:41,064
Antivenom. He can be saved with antivenom.

488
00:28:41,148 --> 00:28:44,317
-Hopps, what did you do?
-No, Hoggbottom.

489
00:28:44,401 --> 00:28:46,444
They're helping the snake.
They're trying to kill the chief!

490
00:28:46,528 --> 00:28:48,280
-Hoggbottom, no. Please.
-Backup! I need backup!

491
00:28:48,363 --> 00:28:49,947
-My page. Let's go.
-Wait, wait, wait.

492
00:28:50,032 --> 00:28:52,367
I need the journal and probably a sweater.

493
00:28:56,121 --> 00:28:57,164
Cold.

494
00:29:00,667 --> 00:29:02,044
-On the ground.
-Wait.

495
00:29:03,295 --> 00:29:05,463
-Oi!
-Next time, we stay home?

496
00:29:05,547 --> 00:29:06,923
Don't you move.

497
00:29:10,760 --> 00:29:12,054
No. Stop them!

498
00:29:12,137 --> 00:29:13,388
-Oi!
-Come on.

499
00:29:13,471 --> 00:29:14,597
-Stop!
-Come on!

500
00:29:14,681 --> 00:29:15,973
Stop!

501
00:29:16,058 --> 00:29:17,059
Oh, God.

502
00:29:20,312 --> 00:29:22,605
<i>I really am just a dumb bunny.</i>

503
00:29:22,689 --> 00:29:24,816
Accident. Your words, not mine.

504
00:29:34,284 --> 00:29:36,328
Put 'em in the trunk.

505
00:29:47,922 --> 00:29:50,175
{\an8}"Ter-roar" in Tundratown.

506
00:29:50,258 --> 00:29:52,135
{\an8}A fanging at the fiesta.

507
00:29:52,219 --> 00:29:56,514
{\an8}A howl-a at the gala as a venomous snake
attacks the founding family.

508
00:29:56,598 --> 00:29:58,516
{\an8}More shocking, the snake was aided

509
00:29:58,600 --> 00:30:02,020
{\an8}by embattled ZPD officers
Nicholas Wilde and Judy Hopps.

510
00:30:02,104 --> 00:30:04,564
{\an8}All three now suspects
in the tragic fanging

511
00:30:04,647 --> 00:30:08,943
{\an8}of Zootopia's chief of police
and considered extremely dangerous.

512
00:30:09,902 --> 00:30:13,448
Chief Bogo… is dead.

513
00:30:15,283 --> 00:30:17,327
Those are words I'm glad
I don't have to say.

514
00:30:18,370 --> 00:30:19,371
I might have to soon.

515
00:30:19,454 --> 00:30:21,456
The doctors are a little weirded out,

516
00:30:21,539 --> 00:30:26,169
but what has happened tonight
was more than horseplay.

517
00:30:31,341 --> 00:30:32,634
We…

518
00:30:32,717 --> 00:30:34,636
We're gonna take that snake down.

519
00:30:35,137 --> 00:30:38,098
And the fox and that rabbit!

520
00:30:39,391 --> 00:30:42,644
Do you know how many mayors
they've already destroyed?

521
00:30:44,854 --> 00:30:49,234
One, two… I got up to two.

522
00:30:49,317 --> 00:30:51,319
Would you like to be "three"?

523
00:30:52,404 --> 00:30:54,906
I want them gone.

524
00:30:55,573 --> 00:30:58,993
But aren't there… laws?

525
00:30:59,827 --> 00:31:03,831
I will say this once, Brian.

526
00:31:04,374 --> 00:31:09,337
You will retrieve the journal
and bury them.

527
00:31:10,547 --> 00:31:13,966
Or maybe I chose the wrong mayor.

528
00:31:15,927 --> 00:31:18,763
Did I choose the wrong mayor?

529
00:32:08,813 --> 00:32:09,647
Hey.

530
00:32:09,731 --> 00:32:12,567
The phone is the first thing
they'll track.

531
00:32:13,443 --> 00:32:19,366
Sorry. I have never been
on the run from the law before.

532
00:32:19,449 --> 00:32:20,700
Yeah. Boy.

533
00:32:20,783 --> 00:32:23,745
It's almost like we shouldn't
have gone to that gala, huh?

534
00:32:24,496 --> 00:32:26,248
Okie doke. We already did it your way.

535
00:32:26,331 --> 00:32:28,583
With Mr. Big, we do it mine.

536
00:32:28,666 --> 00:32:32,670
This is where having a partner from
the underworld is really gonna pay off.

537
00:32:33,713 --> 00:32:35,965
Raymond! And is that Kev…

538
00:32:59,656 --> 00:33:02,325
Welcome to my warehouse.

539
00:33:02,409 --> 00:33:03,910
Oh, my God, I love your dress.

540
00:33:04,577 --> 00:33:05,995
Thank you.

541
00:33:06,579 --> 00:33:10,333
Fru Fru and I are now equal partners
in the family business.

542
00:33:10,833 --> 00:33:14,921
She brings wonderful ideas
for crime and for fashion

543
00:33:15,004 --> 00:33:18,883
while I get more time to focus
on what matters most:

544
00:33:19,509 --> 00:33:22,304
being grandpapa to my little Judith.

545
00:33:22,387 --> 00:33:24,056
Kiss my ring.

546
00:33:24,639 --> 00:33:27,309
-Kiss the stinking ring right now!
-You kiss it right now! Kiss it!

547
00:33:29,269 --> 00:33:33,940
<i>Nonno</i>, you said we were gonna
make cement shoes for Mr. Weaselton.

548
00:33:34,024 --> 00:33:35,358
That's my girl.

549
00:33:35,442 --> 00:33:37,069
They grow up so fast.

550
00:33:37,152 --> 00:33:39,279
-You shut your mouth, fox!
-You run your mouth too much!

551
00:33:40,238 --> 00:33:42,282
Anyways, you're in trouble.

552
00:33:42,365 --> 00:33:45,993
And we like one of yas,
so we're here to help.

553
00:33:46,078 --> 00:33:48,538
New clothes. New identities.

554
00:33:48,621 --> 00:33:50,540
Truck out of town leaves in an hour.

555
00:33:50,623 --> 00:33:52,750
And your meter maid cart is taken care of.

556
00:33:56,421 --> 00:33:58,173
I think you misunderstand.

557
00:33:58,256 --> 00:34:01,634
We've been framed by the lynxes.
And an innocent snake needs…

558
00:34:01,718 --> 00:34:07,182
Judy, the lynxes are killers,
and they have no honor.

559
00:34:07,265 --> 00:34:11,769
And this Tundratown expansion
only makes them more dangerous.

560
00:34:11,853 --> 00:34:16,108
Territorial animals will do anything
for more land.

561
00:34:16,191 --> 00:34:21,363
So, if whatever you're tangled up in
threatens their plans, you're dead.

562
00:34:21,446 --> 00:34:24,782
You don't fight the cats. You leave.

563
00:34:24,866 --> 00:34:27,952
Sir, I know you're trying
to look out for us.

564
00:34:28,036 --> 00:34:30,705
But we took an oath to protect this city.

565
00:34:30,788 --> 00:34:32,499
And justice doesn't run.

566
00:34:32,582 --> 00:34:38,296
On behalf of myself, "Rick Wilde,"
and "Miss Trudy Cabbagepatch,"

567
00:34:38,380 --> 00:34:40,423
we thank you for your truck
to salvation and--

568
00:34:40,507 --> 00:34:41,716
We cannot accept your offer.

569
00:34:41,799 --> 00:34:43,468
We are helping that snake.

570
00:34:43,551 --> 00:34:45,178
Boy, you're really
tossing that "we" around.

571
00:34:45,262 --> 00:34:47,889
Snakes may have been framed
for the fanging of that tortoise.

572
00:34:47,972 --> 00:34:49,641
Do you have any idea why?

573
00:34:49,724 --> 00:34:52,102
Or how this book could prove it?

574
00:34:52,185 --> 00:34:53,686
I wish I did.

575
00:34:54,229 --> 00:34:59,526
But if it has to do with snakes,
your best bet is talking to a reptile.

576
00:35:00,277 --> 00:35:01,903
Do you know any?

577
00:35:02,487 --> 00:35:04,072
Almost no one does.

578
00:35:04,156 --> 00:35:08,285
They keep to themselves,
hide out in Marsh Market.

579
00:35:08,368 --> 00:35:10,203
But you can still find 'em.

580
00:35:10,287 --> 00:35:12,705
You just need a reptile expert.

581
00:35:12,789 --> 00:35:15,250
{\an8}Someone local who knows where to go.

582
00:35:15,333 --> 00:35:17,377
{\an8}Lucky for you, I met someone.

583
00:35:17,460 --> 00:35:18,545
She runs a podcast!

584
00:35:19,837 --> 00:35:23,341
No, no. Trudy Cabbagepatch,
Rick Wilde says no.

585
00:35:24,342 --> 00:35:26,219
Oh, yeah!

586
00:35:27,554 --> 00:35:32,475
If Hopps and Wilde are on the lam,
subtle sleuthin' 'tis my jam.

587
00:35:33,101 --> 00:35:34,436
Mind the splinters.

588
00:35:34,519 --> 00:35:36,063
You called the right beaver.

589
00:35:36,146 --> 00:35:39,191
If you wanna talk to a reptile,
I am your gal.

590
00:35:39,274 --> 00:35:44,112
Also, if you need to talk to a jackalope,
which are real, Mom!

591
00:35:44,779 --> 00:35:46,364
Excuse us. Team meeting.

592
00:35:46,448 --> 00:35:47,949
Vetoing the beave.

593
00:35:48,033 --> 00:35:50,493
New vote: All in favor
of "Truck to Being Alive

594
00:35:50,577 --> 00:35:53,288
and Laying Low
Till This Blows Over-ville"? I mean…

595
00:35:53,371 --> 00:35:55,290
Hopps and Wilde do not bail on a case.

596
00:35:55,373 --> 00:35:56,374
But she's crazy.

597
00:35:56,458 --> 00:35:59,001
Probably lead poisoning
from eating too many pencils.

598
00:36:01,838 --> 00:36:04,091
Do you know a reptile who can
tell us the hidden secret in here,

599
00:36:04,174 --> 00:36:05,842
or why the lynxes framed the snakes?

600
00:36:07,719 --> 00:36:09,971
Twenty-four hours. Sunrise tomorrow.

601
00:36:10,055 --> 00:36:13,641
If we strike out, then we go into hiding,
and "Judy Cabbagepatch" it is.

602
00:36:15,935 --> 00:36:19,272
It's "Trudy" Cabbagepatch,
and the safe word is "coconut."

603
00:36:19,356 --> 00:36:23,151
In the darkness of a dank underpass,
they stepped into the vehicle,

604
00:36:23,235 --> 00:36:26,696
not realizing their lives
were about to change.

605
00:36:26,779 --> 00:36:28,948
Because we're gonna meet a reptile?

606
00:36:29,032 --> 00:36:34,079
Because you're about to be best buds
with Nibbles Maplestick!

607
00:36:34,162 --> 00:36:37,749
Takes two to tango,
but a threesome to be somethin'.

608
00:36:37,832 --> 00:36:41,253
Let's find you a reptile
and romp the swamp.

609
00:36:49,719 --> 00:36:51,679
Breathe it in!

610
00:36:56,643 --> 00:36:59,062
Now, if any reptile would know
the secret of that book

611
00:36:59,146 --> 00:37:00,522
and why the snake wants it...

612
00:37:00,605 --> 00:37:01,523
Hey, Johnny!

613
00:37:01,606 --> 00:37:02,440
Oh, no!

614
00:37:02,524 --> 00:37:04,984
...it's my old lizard friend, Jesús.

615
00:37:05,068 --> 00:37:08,155
Why would a lizard hide here?

616
00:37:09,947 --> 00:37:11,866
Well, when Zootopia was founded,

617
00:37:11,949 --> 00:37:15,245
the whole neighborhood got cut off
from the rest of the town.

618
00:37:15,328 --> 00:37:16,704
Kinda on our own here.

619
00:37:17,705 --> 00:37:19,874
Yeah, that's probably why
reptiles like it.

620
00:37:19,957 --> 00:37:21,626
They can just do their thing.

621
00:37:21,709 --> 00:37:23,086
Plus, it's the only place in town

622
00:37:23,170 --> 00:37:26,131
you can get away with
wearing a shirt and no pants.

623
00:37:26,214 --> 00:37:29,008
Loving tour time with Woodchuckles.
Great call.

624
00:37:29,801 --> 00:37:33,430
-We need her.
-Oh, yeah. Like a hole in the head.

625
00:37:33,513 --> 00:37:34,514
Bad timing.

626
00:37:35,807 --> 00:37:39,602
Okay, to get to Jesús's hideout,
we gotta take a ferry.

627
00:37:39,686 --> 00:37:41,021
Just let me handle the locals.

628
00:37:41,104 --> 00:37:43,856
Water folk tend to be
a little standoffish around landies.

629
00:37:43,940 --> 00:37:46,359
Well, I've been hustling the streets
since I was 12.

630
00:37:46,443 --> 00:37:48,778
I think I can handle a juggling seal.

631
00:37:50,738 --> 00:37:52,574
Sea lion.

632
00:37:52,657 --> 00:37:53,741
He's not from around here.

633
00:37:53,825 --> 00:37:55,535
But coin's a coin.

634
00:37:55,618 --> 00:37:59,247
No. Change is a choking hazard.

635
00:37:59,331 --> 00:38:00,498
Well, honest mistake.

636
00:38:02,375 --> 00:38:03,876
Do not do that!

637
00:38:03,960 --> 00:38:05,503
Let's let Nibbles handle this.

638
00:38:05,587 --> 00:38:07,089
Thank you, Judith.

639
00:38:07,172 --> 00:38:09,716
Conversing with these
beautiful sea creatures

640
00:38:09,799 --> 00:38:11,718
is like talking to anyone
a little different.

641
00:38:11,801 --> 00:38:15,638
Just takes open
and respectful communication.

642
00:38:24,022 --> 00:38:25,023
-Heya, bub.
-Hey, bub.

643
00:38:25,107 --> 00:38:26,399
-Heya, bub, hey.
-Hey, bub, heya.

644
00:38:26,483 --> 00:38:27,650
-Bubba, bubba, bubba.
-Bubba, bubba, bubba.

645
00:38:27,734 --> 00:38:29,652
-Heya, bub.
-Hey, bub. Hey, bub, bub.

646
00:38:29,736 --> 00:38:31,821
-Heya, bub. Bubby, bub.
-Hey, bub. Hey, bub. Hey, ho.

647
00:38:31,904 --> 00:38:32,822
-Heya, bub, bub.
-Hey, bub.

648
00:38:33,865 --> 00:38:34,907
Seen Jesús?

649
00:38:34,991 --> 00:38:35,992
Yup.

650
00:38:42,457 --> 00:38:44,709
And now we leave town.

651
00:39:00,100 --> 00:39:01,684
Thank you for this.

652
00:39:02,477 --> 00:39:03,936
Happy anniversary.

653
00:39:04,937 --> 00:39:06,231
It's your anniversary?

654
00:39:07,607 --> 00:39:10,527
<i>This is the night</i>

655
00:39:10,610 --> 00:39:13,613
<i>It's a beautiful night</i>

656
00:39:13,696 --> 00:39:19,411
<i>And we call it bella notte</i>

657
00:39:26,084 --> 00:39:28,002
How are we gonna find them now?

658
00:39:33,758 --> 00:39:35,010
Thanks, seal.

659
00:39:36,928 --> 00:39:38,888
Friend. No, no, no!

660
00:39:38,971 --> 00:39:40,432
I'm sorry. I'm sorry!

661
00:39:42,600 --> 00:39:44,269
Just a little farther.

662
00:39:44,352 --> 00:39:46,896
Jesús and all the answers you'll ever need

663
00:39:46,979 --> 00:39:51,359
are right here at the end of this
very scary, dark hallway.

664
00:39:51,443 --> 00:39:55,613
Boy, this is so much better than
sitting on a beach on Outback Island,

665
00:39:55,697 --> 00:39:57,449
sipping piña koalas.

666
00:39:58,200 --> 00:40:03,413
We are not skipping town when there is
an innocent snake out there who needs us.

667
00:40:03,496 --> 00:40:06,791
He's got help: his motorcycle buddy.

668
00:40:07,959 --> 00:40:10,628
You are acting weird.
What is your problem?

669
00:40:10,712 --> 00:40:12,880
Well, A, you railroaded me.

670
00:40:12,964 --> 00:40:14,091
And B,

671
00:40:14,591 --> 00:40:16,093
if you must know,

672
00:40:16,176 --> 00:40:20,513
I have an aversion to reptiles.

673
00:40:21,389 --> 00:40:22,390
What?

674
00:40:22,474 --> 00:40:26,311
Is my discomfort hilarious to you?

675
00:40:26,394 --> 00:40:29,106
No, you're... I'm sorry.
You're right, we are partners.

676
00:40:29,189 --> 00:40:33,110
And whenever I'm uncomfortable, you are
always very considerate of my feelings.

677
00:40:33,193 --> 00:40:34,027
Look out! Snake skin!

678
00:40:35,612 --> 00:40:37,114
I'm slipping! I'm slipping! Hold the rail!

679
00:40:37,197 --> 00:40:38,448
-I'm holding the rail.
-Hold it. Hold it.

680
00:40:38,531 --> 00:40:39,699
-Stop pulling my ears!
-Hold the rail.

681
00:40:39,782 --> 00:40:40,617
Never pull a bunny's ears!

682
00:40:40,700 --> 00:40:42,410
-Hold the rail.
-Never pull a bunny's ears!

683
00:40:45,330 --> 00:40:47,249
I think the real mystery

684
00:40:47,874 --> 00:40:49,876
is how you two ever solved anything.

685
00:40:51,586 --> 00:40:54,756
Anyhoo, let's see what Jesús has to say
about your journal.

686
00:40:54,839 --> 00:40:56,716
And if he offers you food, accept.

687
00:40:57,842 --> 00:41:01,013
Declining is a sign of disrespect,
to all of them.

688
00:41:01,096 --> 00:41:02,639
"All of them"?

689
00:41:26,538 --> 00:41:27,539
Order up.

690
00:41:29,041 --> 00:41:30,542
Hey, another drink.

691
00:41:37,006 --> 00:41:38,550
So warm.

692
00:41:41,428 --> 00:41:42,845
Whoops.

693
00:41:42,929 --> 00:41:43,930
No!

694
00:41:46,224 --> 00:41:47,350
Keep it.

695
00:41:51,313 --> 00:41:53,523
Jesús!

696
00:41:53,606 --> 00:41:57,069
Allow me to introduce my friends.
This is Judith and Nicholas.

697
00:41:57,152 --> 00:41:58,528
They are wanted fugitives

698
00:41:58,611 --> 00:42:01,323
investigating a snake mystery
and trying not to get murdered.

699
00:42:01,406 --> 00:42:02,740
May we sit?

700
00:42:06,828 --> 00:42:07,829
Okay.

701
00:42:14,336 --> 00:42:16,338
I'm good, but these guys look hungry.

702
00:42:20,633 --> 00:42:22,052
Thank you for meeting with us.

703
00:42:22,135 --> 00:42:25,388
We believe snakes were framed
for something in this journal,

704
00:42:25,472 --> 00:42:29,184
-perhaps pertaining to...
-Eat first, talk second.

705
00:43:06,263 --> 00:43:08,265
Hey, guys. They ate it!

706
00:43:09,766 --> 00:43:10,975
Look at their faces.

707
00:43:11,476 --> 00:43:12,935
I don't eat grubs.

708
00:43:13,020 --> 00:43:15,063
<i>Hermano</i>, get me a scone!

709
00:43:16,689 --> 00:43:17,982
Okay, okay.

710
00:43:20,943 --> 00:43:22,404
Metal cover.

711
00:43:23,363 --> 00:43:25,115
And what does that mean?

712
00:43:25,198 --> 00:43:26,199
It means

713
00:43:27,534 --> 00:43:28,576
it's fancy.

714
00:43:29,702 --> 00:43:32,664
It also means
the secret you're looking for

715
00:43:32,747 --> 00:43:34,707
is probably right on the cover.

716
00:43:35,292 --> 00:43:38,211
Somehow vipers can see things
in the metal,

717
00:43:38,295 --> 00:43:40,213
even under the paint.

718
00:43:41,131 --> 00:43:44,676
This is Ebenezer Lynxley's original
journal for the weather walls.

719
00:43:44,759 --> 00:43:48,471
Why would he hide a secret for a snake?

720
00:43:48,555 --> 00:43:49,722
I don't know.

721
00:43:49,806 --> 00:43:53,810
But it must have been big,
'cause a tortoise died for it.

722
00:43:54,311 --> 00:43:57,605
I am guessing that it was a slow death.

723
00:44:01,568 --> 00:44:05,447
The fanging didn't just change
how folks saw snakes.

724
00:44:05,530 --> 00:44:08,825
No mammals trusted any reptile after that.

725
00:44:10,118 --> 00:44:12,745
{\an8}That's why we all left.

726
00:44:12,829 --> 00:44:17,084
And that's when the Lynxleys
expanded Tundratown the first time.

727
00:44:18,543 --> 00:44:20,628
And it's happening again.

728
00:44:21,838 --> 00:44:24,049
The expansion is going here.

729
00:44:24,966 --> 00:44:27,594
Marsh Market will be buried in snow.

730
00:44:28,720 --> 00:44:30,347
We'll all get pushed out.

731
00:44:31,306 --> 00:44:33,516
Those lynxes will get more land,

732
00:44:34,226 --> 00:44:36,894
and there's no way to stop 'em.

733
00:44:42,734 --> 00:44:46,154
What if the secret hidden in here could?

734
00:44:47,155 --> 00:44:48,615
Then I would say

735
00:44:48,698 --> 00:44:53,536
holding it puts the two of you
in grave danger.

736
00:45:01,253 --> 00:45:02,337
They tracked you?

737
00:45:04,797 --> 00:45:05,840
Whoops.

738
00:45:07,759 --> 00:45:09,886
It's the ZPD!

739
00:45:09,969 --> 00:45:12,514
Run!

740
00:45:14,016 --> 00:45:15,517
Follow me. Come on!

741
00:45:18,228 --> 00:45:20,313
Wait. Why are you helping us?

742
00:45:20,397 --> 00:45:22,440
You two saved the city once.

743
00:45:22,524 --> 00:45:24,692
I reckon you can save it again.

744
00:45:24,776 --> 00:45:26,569
And when you do,

745
00:45:26,653 --> 00:45:30,157
maybe everyone will see
we ain't that different.

746
00:45:36,288 --> 00:45:38,165
-Freeze!
-Yeah, freeze!

747
00:45:39,207 --> 00:45:42,001
I'm really sorry. But this is
my family's only chance to come home.

748
00:45:42,085 --> 00:45:43,253
Wait. We wanna help.

749
00:45:43,336 --> 00:45:44,671
Wait. Wait.

750
00:45:45,963 --> 00:45:47,632
Come back!

751
00:45:47,715 --> 00:45:49,592
There's no path. Take the docks!

752
00:45:49,676 --> 00:45:50,760
Wha-- Carrots!

753
00:45:50,843 --> 00:45:52,637
Wait! We believe you!

754
00:45:52,720 --> 00:45:54,097
Nick! Come on! Come on!

755
00:46:05,775 --> 00:46:06,776
Grab it!

756
00:46:12,157 --> 00:46:13,075
Hey!

757
00:46:13,825 --> 00:46:16,244
I got eyes on them.
They're heading for the tubes!

758
00:46:16,328 --> 00:46:17,537
In pursuit.

759
00:46:34,512 --> 00:46:35,347
Hey, bubba.

760
00:46:35,430 --> 00:46:36,389
-Hey, bub.
-Hey, bub.

761
00:46:36,473 --> 00:46:37,307
-Hey, bub.
-Hey, bub.

762
00:46:37,974 --> 00:46:38,975
Go!

763
00:46:39,684 --> 00:46:40,560
-Hey, bub.
-Hey, bub.

764
00:46:40,643 --> 00:46:41,478
Bub-bye.

765
00:46:41,561 --> 00:46:42,395
-Bub-bye.
-Bub-bye.

766
00:46:42,479 --> 00:46:43,688
Bub-bye. Bye.

767
00:46:43,771 --> 00:46:45,065
-Hey, bub.
-Bub-bye.

768
00:46:52,405 --> 00:46:55,158
Pro tip! If he goes for the red line,

769
00:46:55,242 --> 00:46:57,077
do not follow!

770
00:46:57,160 --> 00:46:59,537
You will run out of air!

771
00:46:59,621 --> 00:47:01,664
Good luck! I love you!

772
00:47:06,169 --> 00:47:07,170
Sorry.

773
00:47:08,713 --> 00:47:10,298
Cannonball!

774
00:47:13,176 --> 00:47:14,511
That's a red line. Red line!

775
00:47:14,594 --> 00:47:17,014
You're going to run out of air!
Coconut! Coconut!

776
00:47:38,118 --> 00:47:39,619
Anything you need, I got 'em.

777
00:47:39,702 --> 00:47:41,579
Sequels, prequels, requels.

778
00:47:41,663 --> 00:47:43,873
{\an8}Who says the industry's
going down the tubes?

779
00:48:36,468 --> 00:48:37,510
Are you okay?

780
00:48:37,594 --> 00:48:39,429
Yeah. Yes.

781
00:48:40,222 --> 00:48:41,223
You?

782
00:48:41,306 --> 00:48:43,433
I... Well, I've...

783
00:48:43,516 --> 00:48:46,019
I feel like some...

784
00:48:46,103 --> 00:48:48,480
some "sorries" may be in order.

785
00:48:49,106 --> 00:48:50,857
Nick, it's okay.

786
00:48:51,608 --> 00:48:54,236
I know you didn't want to
lose him on purpose.

787
00:48:55,570 --> 00:48:58,073
Not me sorry. No, no.

788
00:48:58,156 --> 00:48:59,282
You sorry.

789
00:49:00,408 --> 00:49:04,996
Well, now we kinda have no way
to find him or stop the lynxes,

790
00:49:05,080 --> 00:49:06,956
so agree to disagree.

791
00:49:08,125 --> 00:49:09,251
But...

792
00:49:09,334 --> 00:49:10,752
we are alive.

793
00:49:10,835 --> 00:49:13,255
So if we can't find him, that's great.

794
00:49:13,338 --> 00:49:16,174
What we can find is a truck out of here.

795
00:49:30,522 --> 00:49:33,191
Box of old matches. Helpful.

796
00:49:33,275 --> 00:49:37,195
Trudy Cabbagepatch, leaving town.
We're done.

797
00:49:37,779 --> 00:49:39,156
Yodelayheehoo!

798
00:49:39,697 --> 00:49:41,824
Dis is veird place for you to be.

799
00:49:41,908 --> 00:49:43,118
<i>Ja</i>, very veird.

800
00:49:43,743 --> 00:49:44,911
Zey fell out of ze tube.

801
00:49:44,994 --> 00:49:46,829
Okay, ve vill call police to help!

802
00:49:46,913 --> 00:49:48,456
Nope! No, no, we're fine.

803
00:49:48,540 --> 00:49:50,625
Yep, we were just leaving town.

804
00:49:50,708 --> 00:49:55,880
Actually, do you happen to know
where this tube goes?

805
00:49:55,963 --> 00:49:57,507
-Copenhoofen.
-<i>Ja, ist Copenhoofen.</i>

806
00:49:57,590 --> 00:49:59,051
Got it. Thank y--

807
00:49:59,634 --> 00:50:02,429
You chew too much. This is not okay!

808
00:50:07,642 --> 00:50:09,852
Where did...
Where did you find these flowers?

809
00:50:09,936 --> 00:50:12,730
The Liebenflower?
Ze tippity-top of ze mountain.

810
00:50:12,814 --> 00:50:14,107
Zis is ze only place zey grow.

811
00:50:14,191 --> 00:50:15,483
Oh, boy.

812
00:50:15,567 --> 00:50:16,568
Is...

813
00:50:17,860 --> 00:50:20,363
Is there anything else up there?

814
00:50:20,447 --> 00:50:22,240
-Nope, there sure isn't.
<i>-Ja.</i>

815
00:50:22,324 --> 00:50:23,616
Old Honeymoon Lodge.

816
00:50:23,700 --> 00:50:26,536
But is closed for long time.
Was hideout for snakes.

817
00:50:28,871 --> 00:50:30,290
Honeymoon Lodge.

818
00:50:30,373 --> 00:50:32,875
How... How do you reach it?

819
00:50:33,668 --> 00:50:35,712
Tourists take ze tram from Copenhoofen,

820
00:50:35,795 --> 00:50:37,964
but ze rope is faster.

821
00:50:38,965 --> 00:50:41,843
Good luck!
Have fun at ze Honeymoon Lodge!

822
00:50:41,926 --> 00:50:42,927
Thank you!

823
00:50:49,517 --> 00:50:53,146
What does the secret have to do
with the reptiles leaving town?

824
00:50:55,107 --> 00:50:57,109
How did it get hidden in the journal
in the first place?

825
00:50:57,192 --> 00:50:58,901
Will you slow down?

826
00:51:01,238 --> 00:51:02,447
How long do you think it's going to be

827
00:51:02,530 --> 00:51:04,907
before Hoggbottom and the ZPD
figure out where we are?

828
00:51:04,991 --> 00:51:07,827
If anything, we should be going faster.

829
00:51:09,037 --> 00:51:10,288
As I always say...

830
00:51:10,372 --> 00:51:13,625
<i>I really am just a dumb bunny.</i>

831
00:51:13,708 --> 00:51:14,542
Nick...

832
00:51:15,168 --> 00:51:17,254
<i>I-- I-- I really am--</i>

833
00:51:17,337 --> 00:51:19,381
<i>just-- dumb-- dumb-- dumb bunny.</i>

834
00:51:19,464 --> 00:51:21,383
<i>Just dumb-- dumb-- dumb bunny.</i>

835
00:51:21,466 --> 00:51:24,802
This was a gift
to symbolize our partnership,

836
00:51:24,886 --> 00:51:27,264
and it is not appropriate for this moment.

837
00:51:27,347 --> 00:51:30,600
Oh, no, I was just hoping
to jot down some ideas

838
00:51:30,683 --> 00:51:32,810
for what we will put on our tombstones.

839
00:51:32,894 --> 00:51:35,688
I'll start. "His partner did it."

840
00:51:35,772 --> 00:51:37,857
What happened to you that you can't
just have a normal conversation?

841
00:51:39,609 --> 00:51:42,737
I will save that for my therapy animal.

842
00:51:43,405 --> 00:51:46,408
-Yeah, you need a therapy animal.
-Oh, yeah.

843
00:51:46,491 --> 00:51:50,120
Well, you need a herd of therapy animals.

844
00:51:50,203 --> 00:51:54,207
Yeah. You need a whole migration
of therapy animals.

845
00:52:27,824 --> 00:52:29,076
Carrots?

846
00:53:11,701 --> 00:53:13,161
Hey, Carrots?

847
00:53:52,159 --> 00:53:53,160
Carrots?

848
00:54:00,708 --> 00:54:01,959
Carrots!

849
00:54:31,031 --> 00:54:33,783
There was a whole reptile neighborhood.

850
00:54:36,661 --> 00:54:37,870
{\an8}Their home...

851
00:54:40,415 --> 00:54:42,667
The lynxes just erased it.

852
00:54:42,750 --> 00:54:44,836
This is what they do.

853
00:54:46,129 --> 00:54:47,130
We have to go.

854
00:54:47,214 --> 00:54:50,883
They push animals out with lies
so they can have more.

855
00:54:50,967 --> 00:54:51,968
We gotta go. Carrots, we gotta go!

856
00:54:52,052 --> 00:54:55,305
-That's what the snake's trying to prove.
-The ZPD is here.

857
00:54:55,388 --> 00:54:56,931
What?

858
00:54:57,015 --> 00:54:58,766
No one will believe us. Not without proof.

859
00:54:58,850 --> 00:55:01,311
Leave it.
They aren't gonna just arrest us.

860
00:55:01,394 --> 00:55:02,562
The lynxes want us dead.

861
00:55:02,645 --> 00:55:04,356
We need it to solve the case.

862
00:55:04,439 --> 00:55:06,816
-Judy, just...
-We have to solve the case.

863
00:55:06,899 --> 00:55:08,901
-Nick...
-I don't care about the case!

864
00:55:11,321 --> 00:55:12,989
Judy, it's not worth dying for.

865
00:55:16,076 --> 00:55:18,703
The world will never be a better place

866
00:55:18,786 --> 00:55:21,789
if no one is brave enough
to do the right thing.

867
00:55:21,873 --> 00:55:26,878
The world is what it is, Carrots,
and sometimes being a hero...

868
00:55:27,879 --> 00:55:30,757
it just doesn't make a difference.

869
00:55:38,223 --> 00:55:39,349
I think...

870
00:55:41,976 --> 00:55:44,521
I think maybe...

871
00:55:44,604 --> 00:55:45,730
maybe we...

872
00:55:48,650 --> 00:55:50,277
are different.

873
00:56:24,936 --> 00:56:27,022
Oh, my gosh,
I think I just took out a cop.

874
00:56:27,605 --> 00:56:28,606
Sorry, sir!

875
00:56:28,690 --> 00:56:31,193
-Pawbert?
-Yeah. It's... you know...

876
00:56:31,276 --> 00:56:33,778
-We're the good guys!
-I told you she's helping.

877
00:56:39,201 --> 00:56:40,327
Come with us.

878
00:56:42,287 --> 00:56:45,415
We have the fox. We have the fox!

879
00:56:46,124 --> 00:56:47,625
They already have your partner.

880
00:56:48,418 --> 00:56:50,420
They can't get you too.

881
00:56:50,962 --> 00:56:53,381
Please. We can stop my family.

882
00:57:08,105 --> 00:57:09,106
Judy!

883
00:57:09,981 --> 00:57:11,066
No, no, no!

884
00:57:13,735 --> 00:57:14,944
I got you.

885
00:57:30,085 --> 00:57:32,420
All right. Here we go.

886
00:57:46,101 --> 00:57:47,310
What?

887
00:58:00,157 --> 00:58:01,533
You're not dead!

888
00:58:02,742 --> 00:58:03,868
She's not dead!

889
00:58:05,328 --> 00:58:06,746
Sorry, sorry.

890
00:58:06,829 --> 00:58:08,540
I just got my motorcycle license.

891
00:58:12,044 --> 00:58:12,960
Nick?

892
00:58:13,711 --> 00:58:15,130
They...

893
00:58:15,755 --> 00:58:16,798
They caught him.

894
00:58:20,593 --> 00:58:24,264
But out here, no one's gonna catch us.

895
00:58:29,477 --> 00:58:31,063
Mayor Winddancer.

896
00:58:37,652 --> 00:58:38,986
Where's the bunny?

897
00:58:39,529 --> 00:58:41,281
I don't know.

898
00:58:41,364 --> 00:58:43,325
But, you know, she's a rabbit.

899
00:58:43,408 --> 00:58:45,160
So maybe you can pull her out of your hat.

900
00:58:46,494 --> 00:58:47,870
I'm not wearing a hat.

901
00:58:47,954 --> 00:58:49,289
He's messing with you, sir.

902
00:58:49,372 --> 00:58:51,291
I knew that. Get him out of here.

903
00:58:52,625 --> 00:58:53,668
No.

904
00:58:57,755 --> 00:59:00,342
Where are they, Mr. Wilde?

905
00:59:03,511 --> 00:59:04,971
Begin the expansion.

906
00:59:05,055 --> 00:59:06,389
Freeze Marsh Market.

907
00:59:06,473 --> 00:59:08,516
-Flush everyone out.
-What? No, you can't--

908
00:59:08,600 --> 00:59:14,106
Water folk, like foxes,
are lesser mammals.

909
00:59:14,189 --> 00:59:19,736
If I say they helped a dangerous snake,
no one will care what we do.

910
00:59:21,654 --> 00:59:24,449
You're going to a dark cell, Mr. Wilde.

911
00:59:24,532 --> 00:59:29,871
Just long enough to read the headline
of your partner's demise.

912
00:59:32,582 --> 00:59:36,253
You should've left town
when you had the chance.

913
00:59:45,095 --> 00:59:46,429
Dad.

914
00:59:46,513 --> 00:59:49,807
Fur from whoever's helping
the bunny and the snake.

915
00:59:52,394 --> 00:59:54,354
Find Pawbert.

916
00:59:56,481 --> 00:59:58,483
Almost to me hideout.

917
01:00:00,068 --> 01:00:01,361
Snake in the hole.

918
01:00:01,444 --> 01:00:02,487
Yeah, yeah.

919
01:00:05,198 --> 01:00:07,575
We shall succeed, Judy Hopps.

920
01:00:07,659 --> 01:00:11,038
We'll stop the Lynxleys
and save your partner.

921
01:00:11,538 --> 01:00:14,166
I'm sorry. You are...

922
01:00:14,249 --> 01:00:16,251
Gary. Gary the Snake.

923
01:00:16,334 --> 01:00:17,794
And your last name?

924
01:00:17,877 --> 01:00:19,129
De'Snake.

925
01:00:19,212 --> 01:00:21,631
Everything is gonna be okay.

926
01:00:30,973 --> 01:00:34,644
Teaming up with you is very exciting.

927
01:00:35,603 --> 01:00:37,147
Don't worry about my fang.

928
01:00:37,230 --> 01:00:39,732
I got my own antivenom pen.

929
01:00:39,816 --> 01:00:41,109
Stops the toxin.

930
01:00:41,193 --> 01:00:43,028
<i>Stab me straight into the heart.</i>

931
01:00:47,824 --> 01:00:49,951
Welcome to my oasis,

932
01:00:50,702 --> 01:00:52,662
away from my evil family.

933
01:00:52,745 --> 01:00:55,790
I like to come here, just kind of veg out.

934
01:00:56,666 --> 01:00:58,293
-Get with all my cat stuff.
-Wow.

935
01:00:58,876 --> 01:01:01,338
-No! That's actually vintage.
-Awesome.

936
01:01:01,421 --> 01:01:03,090
No! If you could just not.

937
01:01:03,840 --> 01:01:04,841
No! No, please don't.

938
01:01:04,924 --> 01:01:07,510
-Wow! Mammals get the best stuff.
-Oh, my gosh. No.

939
01:01:07,594 --> 01:01:09,012
-What's this do?
-Wait, not that.

940
01:01:10,972 --> 01:01:13,058
Turn it off. Turn it off. Turn it off.
Turn it off. Turn it off.

941
01:01:14,851 --> 01:01:17,437
So, we should probably get to the journal.

942
01:01:18,813 --> 01:01:21,816
How did you become partners?

943
01:01:22,859 --> 01:01:25,820
When I found out the journal was going
to be on display at the Gala,

944
01:01:25,903 --> 01:01:29,407
I sent his family an anonymous letter
asking to see it.

945
01:01:29,491 --> 01:01:34,662
Luckily, they make Pawbert work
in the mail room, so he read it first.

946
01:01:34,746 --> 01:01:37,290
I got him smuggled in from overseas.

947
01:01:37,374 --> 01:01:39,542
Seven days in a crate.

948
01:01:39,626 --> 01:01:43,130
But if I fix things for my family,
it'll be worth it.

949
01:01:43,213 --> 01:01:46,466
Then we'll fix things
for your partner too.

950
01:01:51,013 --> 01:01:52,014
Hey.

951
01:01:52,639 --> 01:01:55,600
I know coming with us was hard,

952
01:01:56,559 --> 01:01:58,770
but we're gonna help your partner,

953
01:01:58,853 --> 01:02:01,231
the same way we're gonna help the city.

954
01:02:02,232 --> 01:02:06,403
We stop my family
and show everyone the truth.

955
01:02:06,986 --> 01:02:08,613
Are you ready?

956
01:02:08,696 --> 01:02:10,573
Light the fire.

957
01:02:19,874 --> 01:02:22,877
How much do you know?

958
01:02:22,960 --> 01:02:26,798
Well, that his family was framed,

959
01:02:26,881 --> 01:02:29,592
and there's some secret
hidden in the metal cover

960
01:02:29,676 --> 01:02:32,220
that only a snake can see.

961
01:02:32,887 --> 01:02:37,934
Not just any snake,
a heat-sensing pit viper.

962
01:02:38,935 --> 01:02:41,854
All it takes is a little warmth.

963
01:02:43,023 --> 01:02:46,193
I just... I don't understand.

964
01:02:46,276 --> 01:02:50,155
Why would your great-grandfather
hide a secret for a snake?

965
01:02:50,780 --> 01:02:52,907
He didn't.

966
01:02:53,658 --> 01:02:54,659
I...

967
01:02:57,495 --> 01:02:59,497
It wasn't his journal.

968
01:03:01,041 --> 01:03:02,792
That's the secret.

969
01:03:03,626 --> 01:03:06,754
<i>Zootopia wasn't created by a mammal.</i>

970
01:03:07,672 --> 01:03:11,259
<i>It was created by a snake.</i>

971
01:03:11,343 --> 01:03:13,553
<i>Gary's great-grandmother.</i>

972
01:03:15,763 --> 01:03:20,060
<i>She wanted to make the city a place
where all animals felt welcome</i>.

973
01:03:22,479 --> 01:03:27,567
<i>So she invented her weather walls
to help everyone.</i>

974
01:03:32,947 --> 01:03:35,200
<i>She just needed an investor.</i>

975
01:03:36,368 --> 01:03:37,619
<i>A partner.</i>

976
01:03:39,287 --> 01:03:43,750
<i>But when my great-grandfather
saw what her idea could be worth...</i>

977
01:03:47,587 --> 01:03:51,049
<i>he plotted to steal her plans for himself.</i>

978
01:03:58,265 --> 01:03:59,807
<i>So he committed a murder...</i>

979
01:04:03,020 --> 01:04:04,354
<i>his own maid...</i>

980
01:04:07,440 --> 01:04:09,234
<i>and framed Gary's great-grandmother.</i>

981
01:04:10,985 --> 01:04:13,280
<i>And because she was a snake</i>

982
01:04:13,905 --> 01:04:15,865
<i>everyone believed his lie.</i>

983
01:04:17,367 --> 01:04:22,455
<i>Soon, no reptiles were welcome.</i>

984
01:04:23,456 --> 01:04:25,167
<i>And over time,</i>

985
01:04:25,250 --> 01:04:31,964
<i>my great-grandfather
buried the reptile neighborhood in snow.</i>

986
01:04:35,052 --> 01:04:40,432
<i>And he died believing no one
could ever uncover his crimes.</i>

987
01:04:41,891 --> 01:04:42,892
<i>But...</i>

988
01:04:44,519 --> 01:04:46,979
<i>he was wrong.</i>

989
01:04:49,482 --> 01:04:53,028
<i>Her original patent was saved.</i>

990
01:04:54,487 --> 01:04:56,114
<i>That's what you're looking for.</i>

991
01:04:56,198 --> 01:04:57,824
<i>She hid it in her home.</i>

992
01:04:58,825 --> 01:05:02,704
<i>Find her home, find her patent.</i>

993
01:05:11,171 --> 01:05:14,341
We just didn't know
where her home was buried

994
01:05:15,258 --> 01:05:17,594
until now.

995
01:05:19,096 --> 01:05:21,014
My family's land?

996
01:05:21,098 --> 01:05:26,186
But under all that snow,
how are we gonna find it?

997
01:05:26,936 --> 01:05:27,770
Wait.

998
01:05:27,854 --> 01:05:29,272
Clock tower.

999
01:05:29,356 --> 01:05:32,817
Her neighborhood, the reptile
neighborhood, had a clock tower.

1000
01:05:32,900 --> 01:05:38,073
It lights up like a beacon. If it wasn't
fully buried, maybe we could see it.

1001
01:05:38,156 --> 01:05:39,699
Follow it to her home.

1002
01:05:39,782 --> 01:05:41,743
Except my great-grandfather
turned off the power.

1003
01:05:41,826 --> 01:05:45,122
But we have the plans that show us
how to turn it back on.

1004
01:05:47,332 --> 01:05:52,545
The power switch to her whole neighborhood
was in the original control room.

1005
01:05:52,629 --> 01:05:53,880
All right!

1006
01:05:53,963 --> 01:05:55,132
And where is that?

1007
01:05:55,215 --> 01:05:57,300
Inside the oldest wall of the city.

1008
01:05:57,384 --> 01:05:59,427
The Desert Tundratown weather wall.

1009
01:05:59,511 --> 01:06:02,555
We get inside, turn on that switch,

1010
01:06:02,639 --> 01:06:04,099
light the clock tower,

1011
01:06:04,182 --> 01:06:08,145
-we find her house.
-We find her patent.

1012
01:06:08,228 --> 01:06:10,980
And my family is finally coming home.

1013
01:06:11,481 --> 01:06:13,650
We will succeed, Judy Hopps.

1014
01:06:13,733 --> 01:06:15,943
We shall succeed!

1015
01:06:19,072 --> 01:06:20,240
I found him.

1016
01:06:22,075 --> 01:06:23,576
Get me Hoggbottom.

1017
01:06:27,872 --> 01:06:31,376
It's Nicholas Wilde. Big-time cop, huh?

1018
01:06:31,459 --> 01:06:33,503
I'll see you at the lunch table.

1019
01:06:34,087 --> 01:06:36,631
I'm gonna tie your tail in a knot.

1020
01:06:36,714 --> 01:06:39,717
-Hoggbottom--
-What are you looking at, butthead?

1021
01:06:39,801 --> 01:06:42,595
This is a mistake.
It's a setup. It's the lynxes.

1022
01:06:42,679 --> 01:06:44,222
Listen to me. Please.

1023
01:06:44,306 --> 01:06:46,599
I know we have had our differences, right?

1024
01:06:46,683 --> 01:06:49,894
But I've always respected you--

1025
01:06:50,687 --> 01:06:53,356
You don't respect anyone.

1026
01:06:55,275 --> 01:06:57,944
Phone. It's Milton Lynxley.

1027
01:06:58,028 --> 01:06:59,821
He's got the rabbit's location.

1028
01:06:59,904 --> 01:07:02,324
Whatever he says, it's a lie, Hoggbottom.

1029
01:07:02,407 --> 01:07:03,991
Hoggbottom, listen to me!

1030
01:07:04,076 --> 01:07:05,118
Oh, God.

1031
01:07:07,495 --> 01:07:12,084
Nicholas Wilde!

1032
01:07:13,793 --> 01:07:16,254
What are the chances?

1033
01:07:17,505 --> 01:07:20,967
You know, this is my first time in prison,
and I gotta say,

1034
01:07:21,051 --> 01:07:23,636
their food is on point.

1035
01:07:24,554 --> 01:07:26,098
How are you even here?

1036
01:07:26,181 --> 01:07:27,390
Destiny.

1037
01:07:27,474 --> 01:07:28,891
Plus an arrest.

1038
01:07:29,726 --> 01:07:31,769
But it looks like Judith got away.

1039
01:07:31,853 --> 01:07:35,898
Was that decision mutual?

1040
01:07:37,317 --> 01:07:40,903
Sensing trouble in paradise.
Wanna talk about it?

1041
01:07:40,987 --> 01:07:43,240
What I want is to get out of here.

1042
01:07:43,323 --> 01:07:44,657
Right, your big plan.

1043
01:07:44,741 --> 01:07:48,078
Lay low on Outback Island
sipping piña koalas.

1044
01:07:48,161 --> 01:07:50,955
Except you can't trust koalas,
'cause they got four thumbs.

1045
01:07:51,039 --> 01:07:53,333
Did a whole episode about it
on my podcast.

1046
01:07:53,416 --> 01:07:56,169
It was called "They Got Four Thumbs."

1047
01:07:56,253 --> 01:07:58,296
But you know what?
At least they ain't platypuses.

1048
01:07:58,380 --> 01:08:01,799
Wait. Can you just...
Can you please just let me concentrate?

1049
01:08:01,883 --> 01:08:03,551
Roger that.

1050
01:08:08,181 --> 01:08:09,932
Just talk to me, dude!

1051
01:08:11,351 --> 01:08:13,686
What's the last thing she said to you?

1052
01:08:14,812 --> 01:08:15,813
She...

1053
01:08:16,606 --> 01:08:22,237
She said maybe we are too different.

1054
01:08:23,905 --> 01:08:26,491
And what did you say before that?

1055
01:08:27,117 --> 01:08:29,494
I said the case isn't worth dying for.

1056
01:08:30,577 --> 01:08:31,578
Like...

1057
01:08:32,664 --> 01:08:37,127
like, you know, as in it isn't
worth her dying for because...

1058
01:08:39,086 --> 01:08:44,091
because solitary animals like me
don't have real friends.

1059
01:08:45,260 --> 01:08:48,680
And I... I don't want to lose her.

1060
01:08:50,515 --> 01:08:51,641
So, I just...

1061
01:08:53,518 --> 01:08:54,811
I don't know how to say that.

1062
01:08:57,397 --> 01:08:59,941
You know what, bud? I think you just did.

1063
01:09:01,151 --> 01:09:02,484
Wait, how did you do that?

1064
01:09:02,569 --> 01:09:07,157
Well, I opened my arms,
put them around you and then I squeezed.

1065
01:09:07,239 --> 01:09:10,492
It's called a hug. Do foxes not have hugs?

1066
01:09:12,579 --> 01:09:15,290
The lock. Yeah, that's Beave 101.

1067
01:09:15,372 --> 01:09:17,999
The rabbit's in Desert Dunes. Roll out!

1068
01:09:18,085 --> 01:09:19,751
Hit it. Roll out!

1069
01:09:19,836 --> 01:09:21,878
Your partner needs you,

1070
01:09:21,963 --> 01:09:24,341
and Nibbles Maplestick
is gonna get you to her.

1071
01:09:25,925 --> 01:09:30,305
Hey! It's the fox dad! And the woodchuck!

1072
01:09:30,387 --> 01:09:33,600
Woodchuck? I'm a beaver!
Call me that again--

1073
01:09:33,683 --> 01:09:36,143
-Oops.
-Okay, go! We gotta go! Here we go!

1074
01:09:36,228 --> 01:09:37,644
Get back here!

1075
01:09:39,731 --> 01:09:41,691
Hello, Nicholas.

1076
01:09:42,192 --> 01:09:43,775
I hope you like my new home.

1077
01:09:43,860 --> 01:09:46,071
I wove it with my own wool.

1078
01:09:46,946 --> 01:09:48,240
Jump scare!

1079
01:09:52,909 --> 01:09:54,161
Adios!

1080
01:10:05,798 --> 01:10:06,799
We're free!

1081
01:10:09,261 --> 01:10:12,222
Wheels. We need wheels. No, no.

1082
01:10:13,515 --> 01:10:14,974
No, no, no, no.

1083
01:10:15,058 --> 01:10:16,518
I am sorry.

1084
01:10:16,601 --> 01:10:19,437
I do not think we can get to Judy
in time to help.

1085
01:10:19,521 --> 01:10:21,148
Unless...

1086
01:10:21,231 --> 01:10:25,610
you happen to know the fastest driver
in the history of Zootopia.

1087
01:10:41,918 --> 01:10:45,004
Hey, Flash, Flash Hundred Yard Dash.

1088
01:10:45,088 --> 01:10:47,549
Partner's in trouble.
Need to get across town, ignore the laws.

1089
01:10:48,091 --> 01:10:49,051
No...

1090
01:10:51,594 --> 01:10:53,096
problem.

1091
01:10:53,721 --> 01:10:54,722
Let's hit it.

1092
01:10:57,059 --> 01:10:58,518
Hey.

1093
01:10:58,601 --> 01:11:00,520
-Glad you're here.
-I gotta tell ya,

1094
01:11:00,603 --> 01:11:03,273
I'm not convinced
a sloth is our most reliable--

1095
01:11:04,649 --> 01:11:05,983
Oh, yeah!

1096
01:11:09,862 --> 01:11:11,323
To turn on the clock tower

1097
01:11:11,406 --> 01:11:13,991
and light the way to your great-grandma's
home so we can find her patent,

1098
01:11:14,076 --> 01:11:17,037
we need to reach the power control room
through here.

1099
01:11:17,120 --> 01:11:20,873
-We have to move quickly or we're never--
-We shall succeed, Judy Hopps.

1100
01:11:20,957 --> 01:11:24,419
Look, Gary, if we don't over-prepare,
we're underprepared and I don't...

1101
01:11:25,462 --> 01:11:30,175
My family's been trying to prove we're not
what everyone thinks for a hundred years.

1102
01:11:30,258 --> 01:11:32,427
But even this close,

1103
01:11:32,510 --> 01:11:36,306
they'd never want me to put
the weight of the world on my shoulders.

1104
01:11:36,389 --> 01:11:38,766
Because I don't have any shoulders.

1105
01:11:40,352 --> 01:11:42,520
We shall succeed.

1106
01:11:46,483 --> 01:11:48,360
I got you, rabbit.

1107
01:11:50,570 --> 01:11:53,115
I cannot help Judy
if I don't know where she is,

1108
01:11:53,198 --> 01:11:56,701
which is why I need you to get
on Paul's computer and track Hoggbottom.

1109
01:11:56,784 --> 01:11:58,453
You are an escaped fugitive.

1110
01:11:58,536 --> 01:12:00,872
I could get fired for even talking to you.

1111
01:12:00,955 --> 01:12:03,250
They're gonna put her to sleep! Do it!

1112
01:12:03,333 --> 01:12:04,334
Okay!

1113
01:12:06,794 --> 01:12:08,171
Did you try restarting it?

1114
01:12:09,339 --> 01:12:10,590
Have you tried not being an idiot?

1115
01:12:10,673 --> 01:12:13,218
Hey, Paul. I got you some donuts.

1116
01:12:14,719 --> 01:12:16,429
-Oh, no!
-Thanks, Clawhauser.

1117
01:12:16,513 --> 01:12:18,556
<i>Clawhauser! Location!</i>

1118
01:12:21,684 --> 01:12:23,520
Oh, no.

1119
01:12:23,603 --> 01:12:25,772
I think we're gonna
have to go through the festival.

1120
01:12:25,855 --> 01:12:26,981
Festival?

1121
01:12:32,779 --> 01:12:34,572
The phone's the first thing they track.

1122
01:12:34,656 --> 01:12:36,408
Go! Go!

1123
01:12:39,786 --> 01:12:41,996
-Clawhauser!
-I am working on it.

1124
01:12:42,914 --> 01:12:44,582
-I got it!
-<i>Great, great, great. Now--</i>

1125
01:12:44,666 --> 01:12:45,417
No!

1126
01:12:56,553 --> 01:12:58,096
What festival is this?

1127
01:13:01,474 --> 01:13:02,475
Yeah!

1128
01:13:06,104 --> 01:13:08,148
Move, move, move!

1129
01:13:11,651 --> 01:13:13,778
-Come on!
-Go, go, go!

1130
01:13:14,654 --> 01:13:16,823
We are with you, Judy Hopps.

1131
01:13:19,451 --> 01:13:21,203
We meet again, Zebra.

1132
01:13:24,039 --> 01:13:25,790
Here come the horns!

1133
01:13:28,501 --> 01:13:31,338
She's going for the door.
She's going for the door!

1134
01:13:31,421 --> 01:13:33,381
<i>Clawhauser, we're out of time.</i>

1135
01:13:34,549 --> 01:13:35,550
I got it!

1136
01:13:36,218 --> 01:13:37,969
Desert Tundratown weather wall
access door.

1137
01:13:38,053 --> 01:13:40,472
Desert Tundratown weather wall
access door. Hurry!

1138
01:13:40,555 --> 01:13:43,933
-What is in that wall?
-Power control room.

1139
01:13:44,017 --> 01:13:47,645
But for, like,
some old part of Tundratown.

1140
01:13:47,729 --> 01:13:50,148
I know what they're doing.
They'll ruin us!

1141
01:13:53,860 --> 01:13:56,404
<i>No more tranquilizers. Put them down.</i>

1142
01:13:56,488 --> 01:13:59,532
-Sir.
-Put them down. Now!

1143
01:14:03,245 --> 01:14:04,787
<i>Take the shot.</i>

1144
01:14:04,871 --> 01:14:07,582
<i>Take the shot! Do it!</i>

1145
01:14:36,153 --> 01:14:37,320
Judy!

1146
01:14:42,200 --> 01:14:44,327
-Snake!
-Power control room?

1147
01:14:44,411 --> 01:14:47,164
Five flights up. Come on, hurry!

1148
01:14:47,247 --> 01:14:49,874
That is a lot of stairs.

1149
01:14:49,957 --> 01:14:53,211
Way to hustle, bud, I love ya! I owe ya!

1150
01:14:55,838 --> 01:14:57,757
It's turning on. It's turning on!

1151
01:15:00,718 --> 01:15:01,969
Nibbles?

1152
01:15:05,807 --> 01:15:06,641
Nibbles!

1153
01:15:08,935 --> 01:15:10,145
Snake!

1154
01:15:11,104 --> 01:15:12,605
Cold!

1155
01:15:13,773 --> 01:15:15,192
It's jammed.

1156
01:15:15,275 --> 01:15:17,985
Don't worry about me. Find the switch.

1157
01:15:18,653 --> 01:15:19,904
No, no, no, no. Not these.

1158
01:15:19,987 --> 01:15:22,865
It would be… older.

1159
01:15:39,049 --> 01:15:40,342
Can you see it?

1160
01:15:41,093 --> 01:15:42,927
Did we turn on the clock tower?

1161
01:15:46,264 --> 01:15:47,265
There!

1162
01:15:48,975 --> 01:15:50,352
It's there!

1163
01:15:53,980 --> 01:15:57,525
We did it?
We can find the original patent?

1164
01:15:59,527 --> 01:16:00,737
Nick?

1165
01:16:01,571 --> 01:16:02,614
The door… It's locked…

1166
01:16:03,573 --> 01:16:04,574
How did it lock?

1167
01:16:04,657 --> 01:16:06,868
Nibbles? Open it.

1168
01:16:08,536 --> 01:16:09,746
Nibbles!

1169
01:16:11,498 --> 01:16:13,291
Dang door stuck on me.

1170
01:16:14,417 --> 01:16:15,627
Sorry.

1171
01:16:15,710 --> 01:16:18,296
Last case there was kind of a twist.

1172
01:16:18,921 --> 01:16:19,922
I know.

1173
01:16:23,801 --> 01:16:25,553
Oh, my gosh. I really…

1174
01:16:25,637 --> 01:16:27,805
My heart is pounding.

1175
01:16:27,889 --> 01:16:29,974
I thought you knew. I thought you knew.

1176
01:16:31,351 --> 01:16:32,852
Sorry, partner.

1177
01:16:34,687 --> 01:16:38,775
Hate to leave you out in the cold,
but I'm gonna.

1178
01:16:40,693 --> 01:16:42,570
Please don't be mad at me.

1179
01:16:43,113 --> 01:16:45,157
But, I mean, you get it.

1180
01:16:46,116 --> 01:16:49,494
We've always been
on the same page. Me and you!

1181
01:16:49,577 --> 01:16:51,621
Underdogs, right?

1182
01:16:51,704 --> 01:16:54,874
You gotta prove you're as good
as everyone else.

1183
01:16:54,957 --> 01:16:56,834
That you belong.

1184
01:16:57,752 --> 01:17:02,382
I know, it's messed up,
but this is my chance.

1185
01:17:02,465 --> 01:17:03,925
I have to take it.

1186
01:17:04,592 --> 01:17:06,886
'Cause when I get
to his great-grandma's home,

1187
01:17:06,969 --> 01:17:09,639
and I burn the original patent,

1188
01:17:10,640 --> 01:17:12,767
I'll finally be something in my family.

1189
01:17:13,810 --> 01:17:16,188
And I'll finally belong too.

1190
01:17:16,271 --> 01:17:17,564
<i>Judy?</i>

1191
01:17:18,523 --> 01:17:20,525
Ruh-roh.

1192
01:17:20,608 --> 01:17:21,859
<i>Carrots?</i>

1193
01:17:26,198 --> 01:17:27,865
No loose ends.

1194
01:17:30,118 --> 01:17:31,536
Bye, partner.

1195
01:17:34,872 --> 01:17:36,291
Bye, Judy Hopps.

1196
01:17:39,877 --> 01:17:42,089
Pawbert. Please.

1197
01:17:42,880 --> 01:17:45,967
You can be different than your family.

1198
01:17:47,969 --> 01:17:49,679
I don't want to be different.

1199
01:17:53,766 --> 01:17:54,809
Nick…

1200
01:17:57,229 --> 01:17:58,813
Judy?

1201
01:18:06,363 --> 01:18:07,530
Judy?

1202
01:18:08,240 --> 01:18:09,657
Nibbles, do you see her?

1203
01:18:09,741 --> 01:18:11,493
No, nothing yet.

1204
01:18:12,827 --> 01:18:13,995
Judy.

1205
01:18:14,079 --> 01:18:15,663
Nick.

1206
01:18:16,456 --> 01:18:17,457
Ni…

1207
01:18:21,211 --> 01:18:23,463
We shall succeed,

1208
01:18:24,131 --> 01:18:25,382
Judy Hopps.

1209
01:18:31,138 --> 01:18:33,431
It's okay. Judy sent me.

1210
01:18:33,515 --> 01:18:35,392
She needs to find Nick.

1211
01:18:37,644 --> 01:18:40,480
Yep. Should've seen that coming.

1212
01:18:44,192 --> 01:18:45,735
Judy?

1213
01:18:48,280 --> 01:18:49,531
Nick.

1214
01:18:50,157 --> 01:18:56,038
We… shall… succeed.

1215
01:18:59,082 --> 01:19:00,292
Judy…

1216
01:19:02,210 --> 01:19:04,921
I can't move.

1217
01:19:06,381 --> 01:19:11,011
And you're too cold to help.

1218
01:19:12,345 --> 01:19:13,930
<i>And he's gonna…</i>

1219
01:19:14,889 --> 01:19:15,890
<i>Nick's gonna…</i>

1220
01:19:16,558 --> 01:19:17,684
<i>Judy…</i>

1221
01:19:18,393 --> 01:19:23,023
<i>The world was never meant to be</i>

1222
01:19:23,106 --> 01:19:26,568
<i>on one animal's shoulders.</i>

1223
01:19:29,904 --> 01:19:34,451
That's why my great-grandma wanted

1224
01:19:34,534 --> 01:19:39,247
Zootopia to be for everyone.

1225
01:19:39,747 --> 01:19:44,836
So, we could all help each other.

1226
01:19:46,296 --> 01:19:49,466
I didn't help.

1227
01:19:51,426 --> 01:19:52,969
<i>You did.</i>

1228
01:19:53,053 --> 01:19:57,849
You chose to help me

1229
01:19:59,559 --> 01:20:05,982
and became my best warm-blooded friend.

1230
01:20:11,113 --> 01:20:13,073
Very warm.

1231
01:20:23,208 --> 01:20:26,044
Permission to hug?

1232
01:20:42,977 --> 01:20:46,481
We're gonna save you and save your friend.

1233
01:20:47,982 --> 01:20:49,526
Judy!

1234
01:20:49,609 --> 01:20:51,611
Hey, he's gonna eat that rabbit!

1235
01:20:51,694 --> 01:20:53,571
No, wait! I'm saving her!

1236
01:20:53,655 --> 01:20:54,947
I'm gonna get her the anti-pen.

1237
01:20:55,032 --> 01:20:56,533
Where is she?

1238
01:20:56,616 --> 01:20:59,452
She… She didn't make it.

1239
01:21:00,662 --> 01:21:04,082
But with snake bites, you go fast.

1240
01:21:05,333 --> 01:21:06,584
You'll see.

1241
01:21:17,637 --> 01:21:19,056
No!

1242
01:21:23,518 --> 01:21:27,397
She's gone, Nick. Just get it over with!

1243
01:21:40,743 --> 01:21:42,037
You're done.

1244
01:21:43,080 --> 01:21:44,872
Know when to quit!

1245
01:21:45,873 --> 01:21:47,417
Anti-venom!

1246
01:21:47,500 --> 01:21:50,795
You can save her with the anti-venom!
Throw me the bag!

1247
01:21:52,047 --> 01:21:53,256
Judy!

1248
01:22:10,440 --> 01:22:12,817
Leave it! You're gonna kill us both.

1249
01:22:13,401 --> 01:22:15,403
It's not worth dying for.

1250
01:22:16,613 --> 01:22:19,074
Agree to disagree.

1251
01:22:30,668 --> 01:22:32,587
<i>Stab me straight into the heart.</i>

1252
01:23:03,535 --> 01:23:05,662
-I was trying to get to you, and…
-I thought… He told me that you…

1253
01:23:05,745 --> 01:23:07,164
-I saw you…
-…that you were…

1254
01:23:09,916 --> 01:23:12,502
I'll help your friend.

1255
01:23:13,545 --> 01:23:17,590
Okay, I don't care that we're different.

1256
01:23:17,674 --> 01:23:19,342
You know? I…

1257
01:23:19,426 --> 01:23:22,720
What I care about is you.

1258
01:23:22,804 --> 01:23:24,472
I care about you.

1259
01:23:26,141 --> 01:23:27,225
Okay?

1260
